var e={"respiratory-asthma":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Asthma** is chronic airway inflammation causing variable symptoms and **variable expiratory airflow limitation**.`,`Both halves are needed: typical symptoms that vary, plus measured variable airflow.`,`The four symptoms are **wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough**.`,`India has about 13% of the world's asthma but **over 40% of asthma deaths**.`,`**Severity is judged retrospectively** from the GINA step needed to keep asthma controlled.`,`**Severe asthma** stays uncontrolled despite optimised high-dose ICS-LABA, or needs it for control.`,`**Mild asthma is not low risk**: about 15-20% of asthma deaths occur in mild disease.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Atopy** and a family history of asthma, rhinitis or eczema.`,`Early childhood viral wheeze with **rhinovirus or RSV**.`,`The **17q21 gene locus** is linked to childhood-onset allergic asthma.`,`Indian home triggers: **biomass smoke, mosquito coils and agarbatti**, dust mite and cockroach.`,`Tobacco smoke, obesity and winter smog on poor air-quality days.`,`Work sensitisers such as **isocyanates** in painters and flour in bakers.`,`Drugs: **aspirin and NSAIDs**, and non-selective beta-blockers including timolol eye drops.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Viruses, allergens or smoke first injure the airway epithelium.`,`Injured epithelium releases the **alarmins (TSLP, IL-33, IL-25)**, which start the cascade.`,`Alarmins switch on Th2 cells and type 2 innate lymphoid cells (ILC2).`,`**IL-4** makes IgE, **IL-5** brings eosinophils, and **IL-13** makes mucus and raises FeNO.`,`Within minutes, mast cell histamine and leukotrienes cause bronchospasm, oedema and mucus.`,`A **late phase** at 3-8 hours sustains obstruction, and only steroids blunt it.`,`Years of untreated inflammation cause **airway remodelling** and fixed airflow limitation.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask if symptoms **vary**, wake the patient at night, and follow colds, exercise or smoke.`,`Ask about past emergency visits, **ICU stay or intubation**, and steroid courses in 12 months.`,`Count relievers: **3 or more SABA canisters a year** raises the risk of attacks.`,`**One or more SABA canister a month** raises the risk of death.`,`Ask the patient to **show their inhaler technique** and say how often they really use it.`,`Symptoms better on holidays and worse at work suggest **occupational asthma**.`,`Always ask about **cough over 2 weeks**, fever and weight loss to exclude TB.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examination is often **normal between attacks**, so a clear chest never excludes asthma.`,`Record **respiratory rate, pulse, SpO2, speech and PEF** at every visit.`,`The typical sign is **bilateral polyphonic expiratory wheeze** with prolonged expiration.`,`A **monophonic or localised wheeze** suggests foreign body, endobronchial TB or tumour.`,`**Clubbing is not a feature of asthma**; it points to bronchiectasis or interstitial lung disease.`,`Look for oral thrush, hoarse voice and cushingoid features from steroids.`,`Plot height and weight on a growth chart in every child.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Spirometry with a bronchodilator test** is the first test.`,`Withhold SABA for 4 hours and twice-daily LABA for 24 hours before the test.`,`**Normal spirometry does not exclude asthma**; repeat it when the patient has symptoms.`,`Without a spirometer, use a **2-week PEF diary**, morning and evening, best of three blows.`,`**Blood eosinophils of 300/microlitre or more** suggest T2 asthma and higher attack risk.`,`Chest X-ray is not routine; order it for atypical features or **suspected TB**.`,`In poorly controlled asthma, check **Aspergillus-specific IgE and total IgE** for ABPA.`]},{title:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Diagnose asthma on typical variable symptoms plus **documented variable airflow limitation**.`,`**Confirm the diagnosis before starting ICS**, because ICS removes the variability you need to prove.`,`Assess control with the **GINA four questions** about the last 4 weeks.`,`No yes answers is well controlled, 1-2 is partly controlled, 3-4 is uncontrolled.`,`Assess **future risk separately**: past attacks, FEV1 under 60%, no ICS, smoke exposure.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Never treat asthma with a SABA alone**, at any step.`,`**Track 1 (preferred)** uses budesonide-formoterol as the reliever; Track 2 uses SABA plus ICS.`,`Steps 1-2: **budesonide-formoterol 200/6, one inhalation as needed** (AIR).`,`Steps 3-4 MART: 200/6 **one (Step 3) or two (Step 4) inhalations twice daily**, plus one as needed.`,`**Seek medical care if more than 12 inhalations** are needed in 24 hours (8 at 6-11 years).`,`Before any step-up, check the diagnosis, **inhaler technique, adherence** and triggers.`,`Give a **written action plan**, teach rinse-and-spit, and stop smoke and biomass exposure.`]},{title:`Treatment - acute attack`,points:[`Give **oxygen only if SpO2 is under 92%**, with an upper limit of 95%.`,`**Salbutamol 4-10 puffs by spacer every 20 minutes** for the first hour.`,`Add **ipratropium** in a severe attack, for the first hours only.`,`Give **prednisolone 40-50 mg daily for 5-7 days** within the first hour.`,`Give **magnesium sulphate 2 g IV** over 20 minutes if FEV1 is under 25-30%.`,`Do not give sedatives, routine antibiotics or aminophylline.`,`Discharge on an ICS-containing controller and **review within 2-7 days**.`]},{title:`Next steps, special groups and referral`,points:[`Step 5: add **tiotropium 2.5 microgram, 2 puffs once daily**, and phenotype the asthma.`,`**Biologics** are judged after a 4-month trial; **treat Strongyloides first** in India.`,`**Pregnancy**: continue ICS; uncontrolled asthma, not the drugs, harms mother and baby.`,`**ABPA**: diagnose with Aspergillus-specific IgE and **total IgE 500 IU/mL or more**.`,`Refer if uncontrolled at Step 4 or needing **2 or more steroid courses a year**.`,`Refer after any life-threatening attack, ICU stay, or suspected occupational asthma.`,`Review **1-3 months after any change**, and within 1 week after an attack.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Asthma drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 (100/6 at 6-11 y)`,`Inhaled; 1 as needed (AIR), or 1-2 twice daily plus as needed (MART)`,`Long-term`,`Thrush, hoarse voice; seek care if over 12 in 24 h (8 at 6-11)`],[`Salbutamol (SABA)`,`pMDI 100 microgram, 1-2 puffs; attack 4-10 puffs by spacer`,`As needed; every 20 min for 1 h in attack`,`Tremor, tachycardia, low potassium; never used alone`],[`Low-dose ICS (budesonide)`,`Inhaled 200-400 microgram a day in adults`,`Daily, long-term (Track 2)`,`Thrush, dysphonia; growth slows 0.5-1 cm in children`],[`Fluticasone-salmeterol 125/25`,`pMDI 1-2 puffs twice daily`,`Long-term (Track 2 Steps 3-4)`,`Controller only, never as a reliever`],[`Tiotropium soft-mist (LAMA)`,`2.5 microgram, 2 puffs once daily`,`Long-term, Step 5 add-on`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention, angle-closure glaucoma`],[`Montelukast`,`Oral 10 mg at night; 5 mg at 6-14 y; 4 mg at 6 months-5 y`,`Daily`,`Boxed warning: nightmares, depression, suicidal thoughts`],[`Prednisolone`,`Oral 40-50 mg once daily; child 1-2 mg/kg (max 40 mg)`,`5-7 days adult; 3-5 days child`,`No taper needed; repeated courses harm bone and glucose`],[`Ipratropium`,`Nebulised 500 microgram or 4-8 puffs every 20 min`,`First hour of severe attack`,`Dry mouth; glaucoma if mist enters eyes`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`2 g IV over 20 min; child 40-50 mg/kg`,`Single dose`,`Flushing, hypotension; hospital only`],[`Biologics (mepolizumab, tezepelumab)`,`Mepolizumab 100 mg SC 4-weekly; tezepelumab 210 mg SC 4-weekly`,`Trial 4 months (depemokimab 6 months)`,`Specialist only; anaphylaxis with omalizumab; deworm first`]]},{heading:`GINA 2026 steps - what to prescribe`,columns:[`Step`,`Track 1, 12 years and over (preferred)`,`Track 2 (SABA reliever)`,`Children 6-11`],rows:[[`1-2`,`As-needed budesonide-formoterol (AIR)`,`Step 1 ICS with every SABA; Step 2 daily low-dose ICS`,`Daily low-dose ICS, or as-needed budesonide-formoterol (new 2026)`],[`3`,`Low-dose MART, 200/6 one twice daily`,`Low-dose ICS-LABA plus SABA`,`Very low-dose MART 100/6 once daily`],[`4`,`Medium-dose MART, 200/6 two twice daily`,`Medium or high-dose ICS-LABA plus SABA`,`Low-dose MART 100/6 twice daily; refer`],[`5`,`Add tiotropium, phenotype, biologic; refer`,`Add tiotropium, phenotype, biologic; refer`,`Refer for phenotyping`]]},{heading:`Tests that confirm asthma`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`When`],rows:[[`Spirometry with bronchodilator`,`FEV1 rise over 12% and over 200 mL`,`First test, before ICS`],[`PEF diary for 2 weeks`,`Diurnal variability over 10% adult, 13% child`,`No spirometer available`],[`PEF after 4 weeks of ICS`,`Rise of over 20%`,`Diagnosis still in doubt`],[`Exercise challenge`,`FEV1 fall over 10% and over 200 mL`,`Exercise symptoms, normal spirometry`],[`FeNO (NICE)`,`50 ppb or more adult, 35 ppb or more child`,`Where available; normal does not exclude`],[`Blood eosinophils`,`300/microlitre or more means T2 asthma`,`Phenotyping, poor control`]]},{heading:`Grading an acute attack`,columns:[`Feature`,`Mild-moderate`,`Severe`,`Life-threatening`],rows:[[`Speech`,`Phrases`,`Words only`,`Drowsy or confused`],[`Respiratory rate`,`Raised`,`Over 30/min`,`Feeble effort`],[`Pulse`,`100-120/min`,`Over 120/min`,`Bradycardia, hypotension`],[`SpO2 on air`,`90-95%`,`Under 90%`,`Cyanosis`],[`PEF`,`Over 50% of best`,`50% or less`,`Silent chest`],[`Where to treat`,`Clinic`,`Start treatment, transfer to acute care`,`ICU, prepare to intubate`]]}],remember:[`No SABA-only treatment at any step - every asthmatic needs an ICS.`,`Formoterol relieves and controls; salmeterol only controls.`,`A LABA is never given without an ICS.`,`Variable airflow = BDR over 12% and 200 mL, or PEF variability over 10%.`,`Control: 4 questions, last 4 weeks - 0 well, 1-2 partly, 3-4 uncontrolled.`,`Silent chest or a normal-to-rising PaCO2 means exhaustion, not improvement.`,`Step down only after 2-3 months of control, by 25-50%; never stop ICS in an adult.`,`Theophylline, doxofylline and oral salbutamol syrups are not recommended.`]},updated:[`GINA 2026: 'maximum 12 inhalations a day' replaced by a seek-care prompt - more than 12 budesonide-formoterol inhalations in 24 h (8 at 6-11 years) means get medical care`,`GINA 2026: as-needed low-dose budesonide-formoterol (AIR) added as a Steps 1-2 option for children 6-11 (CARE trial)`,`GINA 2026: oxygen only if SpO2 under 92%, upper limit 95% for adults, adolescents and children 6-11 (was 93-95% adults, 94-98% children); 92% or more at 5 and under`,`GINA 2026: ICS-formoterol added as an alternative to SABA for mild exacerbations in primary care or ED (adults, adolescents, 6-11)`,`GINA 2026: depemokimab (long-acting anti-IL-5, 100 mg SC every 26 weeks, 12 years and over) and biosimilar anti-IgE added at Step 5; trial at least 6 months for depemokimab`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Asthma (GINA 2026)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Heterogeneous chronic airway inflammation with **variable respiratory symptoms** (wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness, cough) **plus variable expiratory airflow limitation** - both halves needed.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**GINA Steps 1-5 in two tracks**: Track 1 (preferred) **ICS-formoterol reliever** - AIR at Steps 1-2, MART at 3-4; Track 2 SABA reliever plus ICS. Severity judged **retrospectively by the step needed**; control = well / partly / uncontrolled.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Atopy, family history, **17q21 locus**, viral wheeze (rhinovirus, RSV), house dust mite, **biomass smoke, mosquito coils, agarbatti**, tobacco, obesity, occupational sensitisers (isocyanates, flour), aspirin/NSAIDs (AERD), beta-blockers incl. timolol.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Allergen/virus -> epithelial **alarmins (TSLP, IL-33, IL-25)** -> Th2/ILC2 -> **IL-4 (IgE), IL-5 (eosinophils), IL-13 (mucus, FeNO)** -> mast cell mediators -> bronchospasm, oedema, mucus -> **variable obstruction**; chronic -> remodelling -> fixed obstruction.`],[`Key history`,`Symptoms **vary, worse at night/early morning**, triggers; atopy; **past ICU/intubation, OCS courses in 12 months**; **SABA canisters (3+/year = risk, 1+/month = death risk)**; technique, adherence, cost; TB symptoms (cough over 2 weeks) before escalating.`],[`Key examination`,`Often **normal between attacks**; bilateral polyphonic expiratory wheeze; RR, pulse, **SpO2, speech, PEF**; **no clubbing** (if present think bronchiectasis/ILD); monophonic wheeze = foreign body/tumour; thrush, Cushingoid signs; height on growth chart in children.`],[`Investigations`,`**Spirometry + bronchodilator test first** (withhold SABA 4 h, LABA 24 h); normal spirometry does not exclude - repeat when symptomatic; PEF diary if no spirometer; **blood eosinophils 300/microlitre or more** = T2; **FeNO 50 ppb+ adult, 35+ child (NICE)**; CXR only if atypical/TB; **Aspergillus IgE + total IgE** if poorly controlled (ABPA).`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Typical variable symptoms **plus documented variable airflow - confirm before starting ICS**: FEV1/FVC below LLN with **BDR over 12% and over 200 mL** (child over 12% predicted), or **PEF diurnal variability over 10% adult / 13% child** over 2 weeks, or **PEF rise over 20% after 4 weeks ICS**, or exercise fall over 10% and 200 mL.`],[`Monitoring control - GINA 4 questions`,`Daytime symptoms over twice/week; any night waking; reliever over twice/week; activity limitation - **0 yes = well controlled, 1-2 = partly, 3-4 = uncontrolled**; assess **future risk separately** (FEV1 under 60%, exacerbations, no ICS).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Steps 1-2 AIR: budesonide-formoterol 200/6, 1 inhalation as needed**; **Step 3 MART 200/6 1 BD + as needed; Step 4 2 BD + as needed**; **seek care if over 12 inhalations in 24 h**. Child 6-11: 100/6 MART (over 8 in 24 h = seek care); AIR now a Steps 1-2 option. **Never SABA alone.**`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Check diagnosis, **technique, adherence**, triggers first. Step 5: **tiotropium 2.5 mcg x 2 puffs OD**, phenotype, biologics - **omalizumab (or biosimilar), mepolizumab 100 mg q4w, benralizumab 30 mg, dupilumab 200-300 mg q2w, tezepelumab 210 mg q4w, depemokimab 100 mg every 26 weeks**; azithromycin 500 mg thrice weekly. Step down after 2-3 months control by 25-50%.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**pMDI + spacer** for all ages and attacks (one puff, slow breath + 10 s hold or 4-5 tidal breaths); DPI needs **fast deep breath**; rotacap - never swallow, check empty; **0-3 years spacer with mask**; rinse and spit after ICS; check technique every visit.`],[`Treatment - acute attack`,`Severe = words, RR over 30, pulse over 120, **SpO2 under 90%, PEF 50% or less**; **oxygen only if SpO2 under 92%, upper limit 95%**; **salbutamol 4-10 puffs by spacer q20 min x 1 h**; ipratropium in severe; **prednisolone 40-50 mg x 5-7 days** (child 1-2 mg/kg x 3-5 days); MgSO4 2 g IV; mild attack - ICS-formoterol alternative to SABA.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy: continue ICS/ICS-formoterol** (budesonide most data); under 5: daily low-dose ICS 3-month trial; elderly: pMDI-spacer or soft-mist; **AERD**: avoid NSAIDs; **ABPA**: prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg or itraconazole 200 mg BD x 4 months; asthma-COPD: always ICS.`],[`Complications`,`Status asthmaticus, **silent chest -> respiratory arrest**, pneumothorax, fixed obstruction, ABPA, **OCS harm (cumulative 500 mg+)**: osteoporosis, diabetes, cataract, adrenal suppression; ICS: thrush, dysphonia, growth slowing 0.5-1 cm.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Drowsiness, confusion, **silent chest**, cyanosis, normal/rising PaCO2; past ICU; 2+ OCS courses/year; uncontrolled at Step 4; occupational asthma; suspected ABPA/EGPA; under-5s needing more than low-dose ICS.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **1-3 months after change**, then 3-12 monthly; **within 1 week after an attack**; pregnancy every 4-6 weeks; spirometry at diagnosis, 3-6 months on controller, then every 1-2 years; written action plan updated; goal - **clinical remission on treatment**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Avoid smoke, **LPG instead of biomass (Ujjwala)**, no coils/agarbatti indoors; annual influenza, COVID-19, pneumococcal, RSV (older adults); weight loss; HDM SLIT if FEV1 over 70%; remove occupational exposure early.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**GINA 2026**: seek-care prompt (over 12 inhalations/24 h) replaces 'maximum dose'; **AIR option for 6-11 years**; oxygen under 92% / ceiling 95%; ICS-formoterol for mild attacks; **depemokimab** and biosimilar anti-IgE added.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**No SABA-only treatment at any step**; India has ~13% of asthma cases but 40%+ of deaths; **formoterol relieves, salmeterol only controls**; LABA never without ICS; montelukast boxed warning (neuropsychiatric); theophylline/doxofylline not recommended.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Asthma in the OPD - diagnose, grade, treat (GINA 2026)`,caption:`Condensed exam algorithm from first visit to the step decision.`,steps:[{label:`Variable wheeze, cough, breathlessness, chest tightness`,detail:`Worse at night, triggers, atopy; exclude TB if cough over 2 weeks`},{label:`Confirm variable airflow before ICS`,detail:`BDR over 12% and 200 mL, or PEF variability over 10% adult / 13% child`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Assess control: 4 questions over 4 weeks`,detail:`0 yes well, 1-2 partly, 3-4 uncontrolled; plus future risk`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Check technique, adherence, triggers first`,detail:`Most poor control is not drug failure`},{label:`Start Track 1 ICS-formoterol at the right step`,detail:`AIR 200/6 as needed; MART 1 BD (Step 3) or 2 BD (Step 4) plus as needed`},{label:`Seek care if over 12 inhalations in 24 h`,detail:`8 in children 6-11; any life-threatening feature - emergency`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Review 1-3 months; step down after 2-3 months control`,detail:`Reduce ICS 25-50%; never stop ICS in an adult`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`GINA 2026 Track 1 ladder (12 years and over)`,caption:`Preferred reliever is low-dose budesonide-formoterol at every step.`,steps:[{label:`Steps 1-2: AIR only`,detail:`Budesonide-formoterol 200/6, 1 inhalation as needed, also before exercise`},{label:`Step 3: low-dose MART`,detail:`200/6 one inhalation BD plus one as needed (beclometasone-formoterol 100/6 alternative)`},{label:`Step 4: medium-dose MART`,detail:`200/6 two inhalations BD plus one as needed; refer if uncontrolled`},{label:`Step 5: add-on and phenotype`,detail:`Tiotropium 2.5 mcg x 2 puffs OD; azithromycin 500 mg thrice weekly; check eosinophils, FeNO, IgE`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Biologics after 4-month trial`,detail:`Anti-IgE, anti-IL-5/5R (mepolizumab, benralizumab, depemokimab 100 mg q26 weeks), dupilumab, tezepelumab`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute asthma - first hour (GINA 2026)`,steps:[{label:`Grade: speech, RR, pulse, SpO2, PEF`,detail:`Severe: words, RR over 30, pulse over 120, SpO2 under 90%, PEF 50% or less`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Drowsy, confused or silent chest?`,detail:`Life-threatening - ICU, continuous bronchodilator, prepare to intubate`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Oxygen only if SpO2 under 92%`,detail:`Titrate to no higher than 95% (adult, 6-11); 92% or more at 5 and under`},{label:`Salbutamol 4-10 puffs by spacer q20 min x 1 h`,detail:`Or 2.5-5 mg nebulised; add ipratropium 500 mcg if severe; mild - ICS-formoterol alternative`},{label:`Prednisolone within 1 hour`,detail:`40-50 mg x 5-7 days; child 1-2 mg/kg (max 40 mg) x 3-5 days; MgSO4 2 g IV if FEV1 under 25-30%`},{label:`Reassess at 1 hour`,detail:`PEF under 60% or SpO2 low - admit; over 60-80% and settled - discharge`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Discharge on ICS-containing controller`,detail:`Finish steroid, action plan, technique, review in 2-7 days`,tone:`good`}]}]},"respiratory-copd":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**COPD** is persistent, often progressive airflow obstruction with chronic breathlessness, cough or sputum.`,`**No spirometry, no COPD**: post-bronchodilator **FEV1/FVC under 0.70** is required.`,`It comes from airway disease (bronchitis, bronchiolitis), alveolar destruction (emphysema), or both.`,`India has about **55 million people with COPD**; it is the second leading cause of death.`,`**Kitchen smoke in non-smoking women** drives Indian COPD as much as tobacco does.`,`Most COPD patients die of **heart disease or lung cancer**, not respiratory failure.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**GOLD grades 1-4** use post-bronchodilator FEV1 and describe severity and prognosis.`,`GOLD 1 is 80% or more, 2 is 50-79%, 3 is 30-49%, 4 is under 30%.`,`The **ABE groups** choose the drug, using symptoms and exacerbations in the past year.`,`High symptom burden means **mMRC 2 or more**, or a **CAAT score of 10 or more**.`,`GOLD 2026 renamed the CAT as the **CAAT (Chronic Airways Assessment Test)**.`,`GOLD 2026: **one moderate exacerbation is now enough** to place a patient in Group E.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Tobacco**: cigarettes, **bidis, hookah and chillum**; count pack-years for every smoker.`,`**Biomass smoke** from wood, dung cakes or crop waste on an open chulha.`,`Dusty jobs: stone quarry, mining, cotton textile, construction and welding.`,`**Post-tuberculosis obstructive airway disease** is a common Indian cause.`,`Low birth weight, childhood asthma and recurrent chest infections impair lung development.`,`**Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency**: think of it when under 45, a non-smoker, or basal emphysema.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Inhaled smoke and dust activate alveolar macrophages and airway epithelial cells.`,`These cells recruit **neutrophils, CD8 T cells and macrophages**, so inflammation is mainly neutrophilic.`,`**Protease-antiprotease imbalance**: elastase and MMPs destroy alveolar walls, causing emphysema.`,`Goblet-cell hyperplasia and gland enlargement cause **chronic bronchitis** with daily sputum.`,`Loss of elastic recoil lets small airways collapse in expiration, causing **air trapping**.`,`Hyperinflation flattens the diaphragm and **dynamic hyperinflation** causes exertional breathlessness.`,`Chronic hypoxia causes **pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale** and secondary polycythaemia.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Breathlessness is **progressive, persistent and worse on exertion**; grade it on the mMRC scale.`,`Ask about chronic cough and sputum; large daily volumes suggest **bronchiectasis**.`,`Pack-years equal **cigarettes per day divided by 20, multiplied by years smoked**.`,`Ask every non-smoking woman about **years cooking on a chulha**.`,`Count **exacerbations in 12 months**: steroid or antibiotic courses, and admissions.`,`Ask about **haemoptysis, weight loss and night sweats** to exclude TB and lung cancer.`,`Ask the patient to **demonstrate their inhaler**; poor technique is the commonest failure.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **SpO2 on air, weight and BMI**; BMI under 21 means poorer prognosis.`,`Signs appear late, so a normal chest does not exclude COPD.`,`Look for **pursed-lip breathing**, tripod posture, accessory muscles and Hoover sign.`,`Hyperinflation: barrel chest, short cricosternal distance, hyperresonance, low liver edge.`,`**Flapping tremor, warm peripheries and bounding pulse** mean CO2 retention.`,`Raised JVP, loud P2, parasternal heave and pedal oedema mean **cor pulmonale**.`,`**Clubbing is not a feature of COPD**; look for cancer or bronchiectasis.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Spirometry after 400 microgram salbutamol** confirms COPD and gives the GOLD grade.`,`Large reversibility, over 400 mL, should make you reconsider asthma.`,`**Blood eosinophil count** decides whether ICS will help.`,`Chest X-ray excludes TB sequelae, cancer, bullae and heart failure; it cannot diagnose COPD.`,`If resting SpO2 is **92% or less**, do an arterial blood gas for oxygen need.`,`**Sputum NAAT** for anyone with cough over 2 weeks, fever, weight loss or haemoptysis.`,`ECG and echo if cor pulmonale is suspected; alpha-1 antitrypsin level in the young.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Stopping tobacco** is the single most effective intervention at every stage.`,`Use the **5 As** at every visit, and refer to the quitline **1800-11-2356**.`,`Switch biomass users to **LPG (Ujjwala Yojana)** or a chimney chulha.`,`Group A: **one long-acting bronchodilator**, a LAMA or a LABA.`,`Groups B and E: **LABA plus LAMA** in one inhaler.`,`Group E with **eosinophils 300/microlitre or more**: start triple therapy with ICS.`,`**Pulmonary rehabilitation** twice a week for 6-12 weeks for Groups B and E.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Still breathless on LABA-LAMA: check technique, then consider **ensifentrine 3 mg nebulised** twice daily.`,`Still exacerbating with **eosinophils 100 or more**: escalate to triple therapy.`,`Eosinophils under 100: add **roflumilast** (FEV1 under 50%, chronic bronchitis) or **azithromycin**.`,`Exacerbating on triple with eosinophils 300 or more: consider **dupilumab or mepolizumab**.`,`Avoid ICS after TB, NTM infection or repeated pneumonia.`,`**LTOT** if stable PaO2 is 55 mmHg or less, for **at least 15 hours a day**.`,`Low-dose oral morphine relieves refractory breathlessness in advanced disease.`]},{title:`Exacerbations and red flags`,points:[`Exacerbation: worse dyspnoea, cough or sputum developing over **less than 14 days**.`,`Give **controlled oxygen to SpO2 88-92%** by 24-28% Venturi mask.`,`Give **prednisolone 40 mg for 5 days**, and nebulised salbutamol with ipratropium.`,`Give antibiotics for 5 days only if sputum is **purulent** or the patient needs ventilation.`,`**pH 7.35 or less with PaCO2 over 45 mmHg** means start NIV now.`,`Rule out pneumothorax, pulmonary embolism, heart failure and pneumonia before blaming an exacerbation.`,`After discharge: rehabilitation within 4 weeks, and **review within 1-4 weeks**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`COPD drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Tiotropium (LAMA)`,`18 microgram DPI, or soft-mist 2.5 microgram x 2, once daily`,`Long-term`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention, angle-closure glaucoma`],[`Formoterol or salmeterol (LABA)`,`Formoterol 12, salmeterol 50 microgram twice daily`,`Long-term`,`Tremor, palpitations, hypokalaemia; may be used without ICS`],[`LABA-LAMA combination`,`e.g. indacaterol-glycopyrronium 110/50 once daily`,`Long-term, Groups B and E`,`Combined LABA and LAMA effects`],[`Triple ICS-LABA-LAMA`,`e.g. fluticasone furoate-umeclidinium-vilanterol 100/62.5/25 once daily`,`Long-term, eosinophil-guided`,`Pneumonia, thrush, TB or NTM risk; rinse and spit`],[`Salbutamol (SABA)`,`100 microgram 1-2 puffs as needed; 2.5-5 mg nebulised in attack`,`As needed`,`Tremor, tachycardia; avoid oral tablets`],[`Roflumilast`,`Oral 250 microgram daily for 4 weeks, then 500 microgram`,`Long-term`,`Diarrhoea, weight loss, depression; avoid if underweight`],[`Azithromycin`,`250 mg daily or 500 mg three times a week`,`1 year, review at 6-12 months`,`Hearing loss, QT prolongation; exclude TB and NTM first`],[`Prednisolone`,`Oral 40 mg once daily`,`5 days, no taper`,`Hyperglycaemia, insomnia, dyspepsia`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate or doxycycline`,`625 mg three times daily, or doxycycline 100 mg twice daily`,`5 days`,`Only with purulent sputum; diarrhoea, photosensitivity`],[`Long-term oxygen`,`Concentrator 1-2 L/min to SpO2 90% or more`,`At least 15 h a day`,`No smoking or flame near oxygen; reassess 60-90 days`]]},{heading:`GOLD 2026 ABE groups and starting treatment`,columns:[`Group`,`Exacerbations in past year`,`Symptoms`,`Start with`],rows:[[`A`,`None`,`mMRC 0-1 and CAAT under 10`,`One long-acting bronchodilator`],[`B`,`None`,`mMRC 2 or more, or CAAT 10 or more`,`LABA plus LAMA`],[`E`,`1 moderate or 1 needing admission`,`Any`,`LABA plus LAMA; triple if eosinophils 300 or more`]]},{heading:`The eosinophil rule for ICS in COPD`,columns:[`Blood eosinophils (per microlitre)`,`ICS decision`],rows:[[`300 or more`,`Strongly favours adding ICS (triple therapy)`],[`100-300`,`Consider if exacerbations persist on LABA-LAMA`],[`Under 100`,`Do not add ICS; use roflumilast or azithromycin`],[`Past TB, NTM or repeated pneumonia`,`Avoid ICS at any count`]]},{heading:`Long-term oxygen criteria (stable state, confirmed twice over 3 weeks)`,columns:[`Measure`,`Prescribe LTOT when`],rows:[[`PaO2 alone`,`55 mmHg or less`],[`SaO2 alone`,`88% or less`],[`PaO2 55-60 mmHg plus`,`Pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale oedema, or haematocrit over 55%`]]}],remember:[`No spirometry, no COPD: post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC under 0.70.`,`GOLD grades (FEV1) describe severity; ABE groups choose the drug.`,`LABA-LAMA is the default; LABA-ICS without a LAMA is no longer encouraged.`,`Eosinophil rule: ICS at 300 or more, consider at 100-300, avoid under 100.`,`Exacerbation: SpO2 88-92%, prednisolone 40 mg for 5 days, antibiotics only if purulent.`,`Mortality savers: quit smoking, LTOT 15 h, NIV, rehab after admission, triple in selected exacerbators.`,`Prognosis is the BODE index: BMI, Obstruction, Dyspnoea, Exercise capacity.`,`Tobacco cessation is the 5 As: Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange.`]},updated:[`GOLD 2026: Group E threshold lowered - 1 or more moderate exacerbation (was 2 or more) or 1 or more needing admission; Groups A and B now have no moderate or severe exacerbation`,`GOLD 2026: new disease-activity section - target a low disease activity state with no exacerbations; a single moderate exacerbation should prompt consideration of escalation`,`GOLD 2026: RSV vaccination lowered to age 50 and over (was 60)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - COPD (GOLD 2026)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Heterogeneous lung condition with **chronic respiratory symptoms** from airway and/or alveolar abnormalities causing **persistent, often progressive airflow obstruction** - **post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC under 0.70** is mandatory.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**GOLD grade by FEV1**: 1 (80%+), 2 (50-79%), 3 (30-49%), 4 (under 30%) - prognosis. **ABE group** chooses drugs: symptoms by **mMRC (2+) or CAAT (10+)**; **Group E = 1+ moderate or 1+ admitted exacerbation (GOLD 2026)**. Pre-COPD and PRISm are at-risk states.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Etiotypes: **COPD-C** (cigarette, bidi, hookah), **COPD-P** (biomass chulha, outdoor air), **COPD-I** (post-TB), COPD-D (development, low birth weight), COPD-G (alpha-1 antitrypsin), COPD-A (asthma); occupational dust (silica, coal, cotton).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Smoke/biomass -> macrophages and epithelium -> **IL-8, LTB4, TNF -> neutrophils, CD8 cells** -> protease-antiprotease imbalance (elastase, MMP-12) -> **emphysema + small airway fibrosis + mucus** -> dynamic hyperinflation -> V/Q mismatch -> hypoxia -> pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale.`],[`Key history`,`Progressive exertional breathlessness (**mMRC**), chronic cough/sputum; **pack-years** (cigarettes/day / 20 x years), bidis; **years at the chulha** in non-smoking women; occupation; previous TB; **exacerbations in 12 months** (antibiotic/steroid = moderate, admission = severe); inhaler demonstration.`],[`Key examination`,`Pursed lips, accessory muscles, tripod, **Hoover sign**, cricosternal distance under 3 fingers, hyperresonance, prolonged expiration, **FET over 6 s**; CO2 retention signs (flap, bounding pulse); **cor pulmonale** (raised JVP, loud P2, oedema); **no clubbing** - if present think cancer/bronchiectasis.`],[`Investigations`,`**Spirometry post 400 mcg salbutamol** (repeat if ratio 0.60-0.80); CBC - **eosinophils** guide ICS, **Hct over 55%** polycythaemia; CXR (TB, cancer, bullae); **SpO2 92% or less -> ABG**; sputum NAAT if TB symptoms; ECG/echo if cor pulmonale; AAT level if young/basal emphysema.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Group A**: one long-acting bronchodilator (LAMA preferred). **Group B**: **LABA + LAMA** single inhaler (e.g. indacaterol-glycopyrronium 110/50 OD, umeclidinium-vilanterol 62.5/25 OD, tiotropium-olodaterol 2.5/2.5 x 2). **Group E**: LABA + LAMA; **triple if eosinophils 300+**. Salbutamol reliever for all.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Dyspnoea: check technique, add **ensifentrine 3 mg neb BD**, rehab. Exacerbations on LABA-LAMA: **eos 100+ -> triple**; **eos under 100 -> roflumilast 250 mcg x 4 weeks then 500 mcg OD** (FEV1 under 50% + chronic bronchitis) **or azithromycin 250 mg OD / 500 mg thrice weekly**; on triple with eos 300+ -> **dupilumab 300 mg q2w or mepolizumab 100 mg q4w**.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Choose by **inspiratory flow** (DPI needs 30-60 L/min); soft-mist or pMDI-spacer for frail elderly; capsule DPI - pierce, fast deep breath, check empty; nebulise LAMA by mouthpiece (eyes); **LTOT 1-2 L/min to SpO2 90%+ for 15 h/day+**; NIV for hypercapnia.`],[`Treatment - acute exacerbation`,`Worsening over under 14 days; exclude pneumonia, PE, HF, pneumothorax; **controlled O2 to 88-92%** (Venturi 24-28%); salbutamol +/- ipratropium; **prednisolone 40 mg x 5 days**; **antibiotic 5 days if purulent sputum** (amox-clav 625 TDS, doxycycline); **NIV if pH 7.35 or less and PaCO2 over 45**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Post-TB / prior mycobacteria: avoid ICS**; heart disease - **cardioselective beta-blockers are safe**; elderly with low inspiratory flow - soft-mist/spacer; asthma overlap - ICS mandatory; advanced disease - **low-dose morphine** for refractory breathlessness, palliative care.`],[`Complications`,`Exacerbations, **type 2 respiratory failure**, cor pulmonale, polycythaemia, pneumothorax (bullae), pneumonia (ICS), lung cancer, cardiovascular disease (commonest cause of death), osteoporosis, depression, cachexia.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**pH under 7.35 with PaCO2 over 45** (NIV), drowsiness on oxygen (CO2 narcosis), haemoptysis/weight loss (cancer, TB), oedema and loud P2; refer: diagnostic doubt, GOLD 3-4 or rapid decline, **under 40/AATD**, exacerbations on triple, LTOT/NIV/LVR assessment.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review 1-3 months after change, then 3-6 monthly; **1-4 weeks after exacerbation**; spirometry yearly; mMRC/CAAT, exacerbations, SpO2, BMI, technique each visit; **target low disease activity - no exacerbations**; re-assess oxygen at 8-12 weeks after admission; BODE for prognosis.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Quit tobacco** (5As, quitline 1800-11-2356); LPG via Ujjwala; **pulmonary rehab within 4 weeks of discharge**; vaccines - influenza yearly, pneumococcal (PCV20/21 or PCV15 + PPSV23), **RSV 50+**, COVID-19, Tdap, zoster 50+.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**GOLD 2026: Group E after a single moderate exacerbation** (was 2); new **disease activity** concept - aim for no exacerbations and consider escalation after one; RSV vaccine from 50; CAT renamed **CAAT**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**No spirometry, no COPD**; grades describe, groups prescribe; **LABA-ICS without LAMA not encouraged**; ICS by eosinophils (300+ yes, under 100 no); **SpO2 88-92%** in exacerbation; LTOT at **PaO2 55 mmHg or less** (or 55-60 with cor pulmonale/Hct over 55%); mortality reducers - quitting, LTOT, NIV, rehab.`]]},flows:[{kind:`branch`,heading:`GOLD 2026 ABE - who starts on what`,caption:`Exacerbations in the past year first, then symptom burden.`,root:`Spirometry-confirmed COPD (post-BD FEV1/FVC under 0.70)`,arms:[{label:`Group A`,steps:[`No moderate or severe exacerbation`,`mMRC 0-1 and CAAT under 10`,`One long-acting bronchodilator (LAMA preferred)`]},{label:`Group B`,steps:[`No moderate or severe exacerbation`,`mMRC 2+ or CAAT 10+`,`LABA + LAMA in one inhaler`]},{label:`Group E`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`1+ moderate (new in 2026) or 1+ admitted exacerbation`,`LABA + LAMA`,`Add ICS (triple) if eosinophils 300/microlitre or more`]}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`COPD follow-up - escalate by the problem`,caption:`Condensed GOLD 2026 follow-up pathway.`,steps:[{label:`Review: technique, adherence, smoking, comorbidity`,detail:`Fix these before changing drugs`},{label:`Main problem: dyspnoea or exacerbations?`,detail:`If both, follow the exacerbation pathway`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Dyspnoea on LABA-LAMA`,detail:`Rule out HF, anaemia; rehab; consider ensifentrine 3 mg nebulised BD`},{label:`Exacerbation on LABA-LAMA, eos 100+`,detail:`Escalate to triple ICS-LABA-LAMA (avoid ICS after TB or repeated pneumonia)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Eos under 100`,detail:`Roflumilast (FEV1 under 50% + chronic bronchitis) or azithromycin 250 mg OD (exclude TB/NTM, check QTc)`},{label:`Still exacerbating on triple, eos 300+`,detail:`Dupilumab 300 mg SC q2w or mepolizumab 100 mg SC q4w`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Target: low disease activity, no exacerbations`,detail:`Even one moderate exacerbation prompts review of escalation`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Acute exacerbation - escalation ladder`,steps:[{label:`Short-acting bronchodilators`,detail:`Salbutamol 2.5-5 mg +/- ipratropium 500 mcg nebulised (air-driven if hypercapnic) or 4-8 puffs by spacer`},{label:`Prednisolone 40 mg OD x 5 days`,detail:`Greater benefit if eosinophils 300+; no taper`},{label:`Antibiotic 5 days if purulent sputum`,detail:`Amox-clav 625 mg TDS or doxycycline 100 mg BD (200 mg day 1)`},{label:`Controlled oxygen to SpO2 88-92%`,detail:`Venturi 24-28% or prongs 1-2 L/min; ABG in 30-60 min`,tone:`decision`},{label:`NIV if pH 7.35 or less, PaCO2 over 45`,detail:`IPAP 10-12, EPAP 4-5 cmH2O; recheck gas at 1 h`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Intubate if NIV fails or pH under 7.25 falling`,detail:`Discuss ceilings of care early`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"respiratory-tuberculosis-ntep":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Presumptive pulmonary TB**: cough or fever for **2 weeks or more**, weight loss, haemoptysis or night sweats.`,`Any chest X-ray abnormality also makes a patient presumptive TB.`,`In PLHIV, contacts, diabetics and the malnourished, **cough of any duration** counts.`,`India has **about a quarter of the world's TB** and a third of MDR or RR-TB.`,`Incidence fell 21% from 2015 to **187 per lakh in 2024**.`,`**Notification on Ni-kshay is mandatory** for every doctor, laboratory and chemist.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Microbiologically confirmed TB** has a positive smear, culture or rapid test such as CBNAAT.`,`**Clinically diagnosed TB** is treated on X-ray, histology or clinical grounds without confirmation.`,`Pulmonary TB includes miliary TB; pulmonary plus extrapulmonary disease is classed pulmonary.`,`**New** means never treated, or under 1 month of anti-TB drugs.`,`**Previously treated** includes recurrent, after failure and after loss to follow-up.`,`By resistance: **Hr-TB, RR-TB, MDR-TB, pre-XDR-TB and XDR-TB** decide the regimen.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`A coughing patient spreads **droplet nuclei of 1-5 micrometres** that reach the alveoli.`,`Alveolar macrophages ingest bacilli, which block phagosome-lysosome fusion and multiply inside.`,`**Th1 cells** make interferon-gamma, which activates macrophages and builds the granuloma.`,`The **caseating granuloma** with Langhans giant cells walls off the bacilli in most people.`,`Primary TB forms the **Ghon complex**: a subpleural focus plus enlarged hilar nodes.`,`Reactivation favours the **upper lobe apices**, where oxygen tension is highest.`,`Cavities hold huge bacillary loads with resistant mutants, so TB is **always treated with several drugs**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about cough, sputum, haemoptysis, **evening fever, night sweats** and weight loss.`,`Nearly half of prevalent Indian cases had **no cough**, so ask about every symptom.`,`Ask about **contact with a TB patient** and whether that patient had drug-resistant TB.`,`Ask about **previous anti-TB treatment**: drugs, duration, completion and outcome.`,`Screen for **HIV, diabetes, tobacco, alcohol** and undernutrition.`,`List drugs that interact with rifampicin: **oral contraceptives, warfarin, sulfonylureas, dolutegravir**.`,`Ask about Aadhaar-linked bank account and social support to unlock Ni-kshay benefits.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **weight and BMI** at every visit; weight decides the FDC band.`,`Admit if **BMI under 14, SpO2 under 94%, RR over 24**, or unable to stand.`,`TB nodes are **cervical, matted and non-tender**, and may form a cold abscess.`,`Chest signs are often few: apical post-tussive crackles, or signs of effusion or fibrosis.`,`Look for gibbus and leg weakness (Pott spine) and neck stiffness (TB meningitis).`,`Record **visual acuity and colour vision** before starting ethambutol.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Upfront NAAT (CBNAAT or Truenat)** on sputum for every presumptive TB patient.`,`NAAT detects TB and **rifampicin resistance** in about 2 hours.`,`Rifampicin sensitive: send **first-line LPA** for isoniazid resistance.`,`Rifampicin resistant: send first and second-line LPA plus liquid culture DST.`,`Smear needs 5,000-10,000 bacilli/mL, so it is now mainly a **follow-up test**.`,`Every patient gets an **HIV test and blood sugar**, plus tobacco and alcohol screening.`,`**TB serology is banned**; TST and IGRA show infection only, not active disease.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Treatment is always **daily, multidrug and by weight band** using fixed-dose combinations.`,`Drug-sensitive TB: **2HRZE + 4HRE, a total of 6 months**.`,`**CNS and bone TB: 2HRZE + 10HRE**, a total of 12 months.`,`Give **pyridoxine 10 mg daily** with isoniazid; 25-50 mg if high risk.`,`Take once daily on an empty stomach; if nauseated, take after a light meal.`,`Notify and link to **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana, Rs 1,000 a month** for the whole treatment.`,`**Never add a single drug to a failing regimen**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Hr-TB**: 6 months of rifampicin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide and levofloxacin.`,`**BPaLM** for MDR or RR-TB: bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid and moxifloxacin for 26 weeks.`,`BPaLM needs age **14 or over**, no pregnancy and no CNS disease.`,`Fluoroquinolone resistant: drop moxifloxacin and give **BPaL**.`,`Others get a 9-11 month shorter oral regimen, or an 18-20 month longer regimen.`,`Refer any **rifampicin resistance** to the DR-TB Centre immediately.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**HIV**: start ART within 2 weeks of ATT; delay 4-8 weeks in TB meningitis.`,`On rifampicin, give **dolutegravir 50 mg twice daily**, and cotrimoxazole prophylaxis to all.`,`**Pregnancy**: H, R, Z and E are safe; **streptomycin is contraindicated**.`,`Use a **copper IUCD, DMPA or condoms**; rifampicin makes the pill fail.`,`**Renal failure**: give pyrazinamide and ethambutol three times a week, after dialysis.`,`Add **dexamethasone** in TB meningitis, and steroids in selected TB pericarditis.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Stop H, R and Z if **ALT is over 3 times ULN with symptoms**, or over 5 times without.`,`Restart **rifampicin first, then isoniazid** once ALT is under 2 times ULN.`,`Blurred vision or red-green colour loss means **ethambutol optic neuritis**; stop it.`,`Massive haemoptysis: lie **bleeding side down** and refer for bronchial artery embolisation.`,`A **gap of 1 month or more** means lost to follow-up; repeat NAAT and DST.`,`Sputum at end of intensive phase and end of treatment; follow up 6-monthly for 2 years.`]}],tables:[{heading:`TB drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Isoniazid (H)`,`Oral 5 mg/kg, max 300 mg; child 10 mg/kg`,`6 months (12 in CNS, bone)`,`Hepatitis, peripheral neuropathy; give pyridoxine`],[`Rifampicin (R)`,`Oral 10 mg/kg, max 600 mg; child 15 mg/kg`,`6 months (12 in CNS, bone)`,`Orange secretions, hepatitis; enzyme inducer, pill fails`],[`Pyrazinamide (Z)`,`Oral 25 mg/kg; child 35 mg/kg`,`First 2 months only`,`Most hepatotoxic; hyperuricaemia and arthralgia`],[`Ethambutol (E)`,`Oral 15 mg/kg; child 20 mg/kg`,`6 months (kept in continuation)`,`Optic neuritis, red-green loss; monthly vision check`],[`Pyridoxine`,`Oral 10 mg daily; 25-50 mg high risk`,`Whole course`,`100 mg daily to treat neuropathy`],[`Bedaquiline`,`400 mg daily 2 weeks, then 200 mg thrice weekly`,`24 weeks within BPaLM`,`QT prolongation; take with food`],[`Pretomanid`,`Oral 200 mg daily`,`26 weeks`,`Hepatotoxicity, neuropathy; never without B and L`],[`Linezolid`,`Oral 600 mg daily (300 mg for toxicity)`,`26 weeks`,`Anaemia, low platelets, optic and peripheral neuropathy`],[`Moxifloxacin`,`Oral 400 mg daily`,`26 weeks`,`QT prolongation, tendinopathy; separate from antacids`]]},{heading:`Adult weight bands for daily FDC`,columns:[`Weight band`,`HRZE tablets (intensive)`,`HRE tablets (continuation)`],rows:[[`25-34 kg`,`2`,`2`],[`35-49 kg`,`3`,`3`],[`50-64 kg`,`4`,`4`],[`65-75 kg`,`5`,`5`],[`Over 75 kg`,`6`,`6`]]},{heading:`Drug-resistant TB - type and regimen`,columns:[`Type`,`Resistant to`,`Regimen`],rows:[[`Hr-TB`,`Isoniazid only`,`6 months R, E, Z plus levofloxacin`],[`RR or MDR-TB`,`Rifampicin, with or without isoniazid`,`BPaLM 6 months if eligible`],[`Pre-XDR-TB`,`MDR plus any fluoroquinolone`,`BPaL 6 months (up to 9)`],[`XDR-TB`,`Pre-XDR plus bedaquiline or linezolid`,`Individualised longer regimen, Nodal DR-TB Centre`]]}],remember:[`2HRZE + 4HRE daily by weight band; CNS and bone TB get 2HRZE + 10HRE.`,`Adult bands: 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 tablets at 25, 35, 50, 65 and over 75 kg.`,`Upfront NAAT with universal DST for every presumptive TB patient.`,`BPaLM = Bedaquiline, Pretomanid, Linezolid, Moxifloxacin for 6 months.`,`Hr-TB = 6 months of R, E, Z plus levofloxacin.`,`ART within 2 weeks of ATT, except TB meningitis (wait 4-8 weeks).`,`Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana pays Rs 1,000 a month since November 2024.`,`Never add a single drug to a failing regimen.`]},updated:[`NTEP adult FDC weight bands corrected to the current five bands: 25-34 kg 2, 35-49 kg 3, 50-64 kg 4, 65-75 kg 5, over 75 kg 6 tablets (old four-band chart removed)`,`WHO Global TB Report 2025: India incidence 187/lakh in 2024 (21% fall from 237 in 2015), mortality 21/lakh, treatment coverage over 92%`,`NTEP National Guidelines for Management of DR-TB 2025: BPaLM/BPaL and longer regimens re-cited to the 2025 national guideline`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Pulmonary TB under NTEP`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Disease due to **M. tuberculosis complex**; **presumptive TB** = cough or fever 2+ weeks, weight loss, haemoptysis, night sweats or any CXR abnormality - **cough of any duration** in PLHIV, contacts, diabetics, malnourished.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Microbiologically confirmed vs clinically diagnosed**; pulmonary vs extrapulmonary; **new vs previously treated**; by resistance - **Hr-TB, RR/MDR-TB, pre-XDR (+FQ), XDR (+bedaquiline/linezolid)**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Droplet nuclei 1-5 micrometres; **undernutrition (largest attributable factor in India)**, HIV, diabetes, smoking, alcohol, silicosis, anti-TNF, steroids, CKD; India **~25% of global TB, ~32% of MDR/RR-TB**; incidence **187/lakh (2024)**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Inhaled bacilli -> alveolar macrophage (blocks phagolysosome) -> Th1 / **IFN-gamma, TNF** -> **caseating granuloma** -> containment (infection) or reactivation in **apical-posterior segments** -> cavity (10^7-10^9 bacilli) -> resistant mutants -> **multidrug therapy**.`],[`Key history`,`Cough, evening fever, sweats, weight loss, haemoptysis; **contact with TB/DR-TB**; previous ATT (type, outcome); HIV, diabetes, alcohol, tobacco; **interacting drugs (OCP, warfarin, dolutegravir)**; pregnancy; social support and Aadhaar-linked bank account (DBT).`],[`Key examination`,`**Weight and BMI every visit** (dose band, nutrition); triage severe illness - **BMI under 14 (under 16 with oedema), RR over 24, SpO2 under 94%, unable to stand** = admit; nodes, apical crackles, amphoric breathing, spine, meningism; **baseline vision (Snellen, Ishihara)**.`],[`Investigations`,`**Upfront NAAT (CBNAAT/Truenat) for every presumptive TB**; **universal DST** - Rif-sensitive -> FL-LPA (INH); Rif-resistant -> FL+SL-LPA + liquid culture; CXR (AI-assisted); EPTB - NAAT + cytology/histology, ADA; **HIV, RBS, tobacco/alcohol** screen for all; no serology (banned 2012), no IGRA/TST for active TB.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**DS-TB: 2HRZE + 4HRE daily FDC** (75/150/400/275 then 75/150/275); **bands 25-34 kg 2, 35-49 kg 3, 50-64 kg 4, 65-75 kg 5, over 75 kg 6 tablets**; CNS and bone TB **2HRZE + 10HRE**; **pyridoxine 10 mg** daily; empty stomach.`],[`Treatment - escalation (DR-TB)`,`**Hr-TB: 6 months R-E-Z-Lfx**. **RR/MDR (14 years+, not pregnant, no CNS): BPaLM 26 weeks** - bedaquiline 400 mg x 2 weeks then 200 mg thrice weekly x 22 weeks, pretomanid 200 mg, linezolid 600 mg, moxifloxacin 400 mg; **FQ-resistant -> BPaL** (to 39 weeks); else shorter 9-11 month or longer 18-20 month oral regimens.`],[`Drug adverse effects & DILI`,`H - hepatitis, neuropathy; R - orange secretions, flu-like, thrombocytopenia, **CYP inducer**; Z - most hepatotoxic, gout; E - **optic neuritis**; **stop HRZ if ALT over 3x ULN with symptoms or over 5x without**; reintroduce **R then H**, omit Z after severe DILI; BPaLM - QTcF, linezolid marrow/neuropathy.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy: HRZE safe**, no aminoglycosides; **HIV: ART within 2 weeks** (4-8 weeks in TBM), **dolutegravir 50 mg BD** on rifampicin, cotrimoxazole; **CKD: Z and E thrice weekly after dialysis**; liver disease - fewer hepatotoxic drugs; child - dispersible FDC by bands.`],[`Complications`,`Massive haemoptysis (**Rasmussen aneurysm**), pneumothorax, TB meningitis, Pott spine, IRIS, DILI, **drug resistance**, post-TB lung disease (obstruction, bronchiectasis, aspergilloma).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Rif resistance on NAAT -> DR-TB centre same day**; jaundice on ATT; visual change on E/linezolid; meningism; massive haemoptysis; QTcF over 500 ms; **no response by 2 months**; severe illness at diagnosis.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Monthly review (weight band, adherence, side effects); **sputum at end of IP and end of treatment**; month-2 positive -> NAAT + LPA (IP not extended); outcomes - cured, completed, failed, died, **LTFU (1 month+ interruption)**; post-treatment follow-up 6-monthly for 2 years.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NTEP Detect-Treat-Prevent-Build**; mandatory **Ni-kshay notification**; **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana Rs 1,000/month**; Ni-kshay Mitra food baskets; TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan; contact screening and **TPT**; BCG at birth.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Current NTEP adult FDC uses 5 weight bands (25-34 to over 75 kg, 2-6 tablets)**; **DR-TB National Guidelines 2025** confirm BPaLM; **WHO TB Report 2025**: India incidence 187/lakh, mortality 21/lakh (2024).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Daily, multidrug, weight-band FDC**; India keeps **E in continuation** (2HRZE/4HRE); never add a single drug to a failing regimen; rifampicin resistance surrogate for MDR (rpoB 81-bp region); dolutegravir doubles with rifampicin; ATT is free and notified.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Presumptive TB to regimen - NTEP condensed`,steps:[{label:`Presumptive TB identified`,detail:`Cough/fever 2+ weeks, weight loss, haemoptysis, CXR abnormality; any cough in PLHIV/contacts`},{label:`Upfront NAAT (CBNAAT or Truenat)`,detail:`Plus HIV, blood sugar, tobacco and alcohol screen; notify on Ni-kshay`},{label:`Rifampicin result?`,tone:`decision`,detail:`Sensitive -> FL-LPA for INH; resistant -> FL + SL-LPA and liquid culture`},{label:`Rif-sensitive, INH-sensitive: 2HRZE + 4HRE`,detail:`Bands 25-34 kg 2, 35-49 kg 3, 50-64 kg 4, 65-75 kg 5, over 75 kg 6 tablets`},{label:`INH-resistant: 6 months R-E-Z-Lfx`,detail:`No isoniazid`},{label:`Rif-resistant: refer DR-TB centre, BPaLM`,detail:`26 weeks if 14+ years, not pregnant, no CNS disease; BPaL if FQ-resistant`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Monthly review, sputum at month 2 and end`,detail:`Outcome recorded on Ni-kshay; DBT Rs 1,000/month`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`DS-TB versus RR-TB regimen at a glance`,columns:[`Feature`,`Drug-sensitive TB`,`RR/MDR-TB (BPaLM)`],rows:[[`Drugs`,`H, R, Z, E (FDC by weight band)`,`Bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin`],[`Duration`,`6 months (12 in CNS and bone TB)`,`26 weeks (BPaL up to 39 weeks)`],[`Key toxicity`,`Hepatitis (HRZ), optic neuritis (E), neuropathy (H)`,`QT prolongation, myelosuppression and neuropathy (linezolid), hepatitis`],[`How to take`,`Empty stomach, once daily, pyridoxine 10 mg`,`With food, once daily; linezolid can drop to 300 mg`],[`Monitoring`,`Monthly weight, vision, LFT if symptoms; sputum month 2 and end`,`Monthly CBC, LFT, electrolytes, ECG weeks 2-24, monthly culture`],[`Exclusions`,`None absolute; adjust in CKD and liver disease`,`Under 14 years, pregnancy, CNS/extensive EPTB, prior exposure over 1 month`]]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Drug-induced liver injury on ATT`,steps:[{label:`Nausea, vomiting, jaundice on ATT`,detail:`Check ALT, bilirubin, INR the same day`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Stop H, R, Z`,detail:`ALT over 3x ULN with symptoms, over 5x without, or jaundice`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Interim non-hepatotoxic regimen if ill`,detail:`Ethambutol + levofloxacin (+/- linezolid) under specialist advice`},{label:`Rechallenge when ALT under 2x ULN, bilirubin normal`,detail:`Rifampicin first, isoniazid after 3-7 days, rechecking ALT`},{label:`Omit pyrazinamide after severe DILI`,detail:`Extend continuation, e.g. 2HRE + 7HR`,tone:`good`}]}]},"respiratory-pneumonia-cap":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**CAP** is acute lung infection acquired outside hospital, with **new shadowing on chest X-ray**.`,`**No new shadow, no pneumonia**: without the film it is acute bronchitis.`,`Hospital-acquired pneumonia starts **48 hours or more after admission**.`,`**Streptococcus pneumoniae** is the commonest bacterial cause at every age.`,`Pneumonia causes about **one in seven under-5 deaths** in India.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**CURB-65 0-1** is low severity and can be treated at home.`,`**CURB-65 2** is moderate severity and needs hospital-supervised treatment.`,`**CURB-65 3-5** is high severity; admit, and consider ICU at 4-5.`,`In the clinic use **CRB-65**: 0 home, 1-2 consider hospital, 3-4 urgent admission.`,`Child 2-59 months: **fast breathing or chest indrawing** is pneumonia.`,`Any **danger sign or SpO2 under 90%** in a child is severe pneumonia.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Organisms reach the alveoli mainly by **microaspiration** of colonised throat secretions.`,`Infection wins when the dose is large or defences fail, as in smoking or alcohol.`,`Macrophages release IL-1, TNF-alpha and IL-6, causing fever, rigors and raised CRP.`,`Neutrophils, fibrin and fluid fill the alveoli, producing **consolidation**.`,`Lobar pneumonia passes through congestion, **red then grey hepatisation**, and resolution.`,`Blood still flows through airless lung, so **hypoxaemia is the best severity marker**.`,`Atypicals lack a usable cell wall, so **beta-lactams do not work** against them.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about the triad of **cough, fever and breathlessness**, and its tempo.`,`Rusty sputum suggests pneumococcus; foul-smelling sputum suggests anaerobes or abscess.`,`In the elderly, **new confusion or a fall** may be the only sign.`,`Ask about recent influenza, which raises the chance of **S. aureus** pneumonia.`,`Ask about antibiotics in the last 3 months and admission in the last 90 days.`,`**Cough over 2 weeks**, weight loss or haemoptysis means TB until proved otherwise.`,`Ask about paddy fields, floods and eschars: melioidosis, leptospirosis and scrub typhus.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **RR for a full minute, pulse, BP, temperature and SpO2** on room air.`,`Test mental state: an **AMT score of 8 or less** counts as confusion.`,`Consolidation gives **dull percussion, bronchial breathing** and increased vocal resonance.`,`**Stony dullness** with absent breath sounds suggests effusion, not consolidation.`,`Look for an **eschar** in the axilla or groin (scrub typhus).`,`**Normal chest signs do not exclude pneumonia**, especially in the elderly.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Chest X-ray (PA)** for every adult, but never delay the first antibiotic for it.`,`SpO2 **under 92%** (88% in COPD, 90% in a child) means admission and oxygen.`,`Check **urea**: over 7 mmol/L (about 42 mg/dL urea) scores a CURB-65 point.`,`**CRP in the OPD**: under 20 no antibiotic, 20-100 consider delayed, over 100 treat.`,`Send **sputum NAAT for TB** if cough lasts 2 weeks or more.`,`In hospital: **blood cultures before antibiotics**, urinary antigens and an HIV test.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Give the first dose **within 4 hours**, and **within 1 hour if septic**.`,`Low severity: **amoxicillin 500 mg-1 g three times daily for 5 days**.`,`Penicillin allergy: **doxycycline 200 mg on day 1, then 100 mg** to complete 5 days.`,`Comorbid outpatient: **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg three times daily plus azithromycin**.`,`Child with pneumonia: **amoxicillin 40-45 mg/kg/dose twice daily for 5 days**.`,`Give paracetamol and fluids; **no cough suppressants or mucolytics**.`,`**Review at 48-72 hours**, and return sooner if breathlessness or confusion appears.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`CURB-65 2: **amoxicillin 1 g three times daily plus clarithromycin** or azithromycin.`,`High severity: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily plus azithromycin 500 mg IV**.`,`Pseudomonas risk: piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV 6-hourly replaces ceftriaxone.`,`Add **oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days** if influenza is positive.`,`Severe CAP only: **hydrocortisone 200 mg/day** for 4-7 days; not in influenza.`,`Switch IV to oral once stable and improving, usually by day 2-3.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Never use macrolide monotherapy in India**; pneumococcal resistance exceeds 30%.`,`Fluoroquinolones are reserve drugs because they **mask TB**; send NAAT first.`,`Persistent fever with stony dullness suggests **empyema**; tap under ultrasound.`,`Pleural fluid **pH under 7.2** or frank pus means insert a chest drain.`,`No improvement by 72 hours: think TB, empyema, abscess, obstruction or wrong diagnosis.`,`Necrotising pneumonia after influenza suggests **S. aureus**, including MRSA.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Admit for CRB-65 of 3 or more, **SpO2 under 92%**, sepsis or no home support.`,`**Five days is enough** once afebrile for 48 hours with one instability sign or fewer.`,`**Repeat the X-ray at 6 weeks** in smokers and over-50s with persisting signs.`,`Recovery: fever 1 week, sputum 4 weeks, cough 6 weeks, tiredness 3-6 months.`,`Give **pneumococcal and annual influenza vaccines** after recovery.`]}],tables:[{heading:`CAP drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin`,`Oral 500 mg-1 g 8-hourly; child 40-45 mg/kg/dose BD`,`5 days`,`Diarrhoea, rash; ask about penicillin allergy`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`Oral 625 mg 8-hourly, or 1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`C. difficile, cholestatic hepatitis; take with meals`],[`Doxycycline`,`Oral 200 mg day 1, then 100 mg daily or BD`,`5 days total`,`Oesophageal ulcer; avoid in pregnancy and under 8`],[`Azithromycin`,`Oral or IV 500 mg once daily`,`3 days`,`QT prolongation; never used alone in India`],[`Clarithromycin`,`Oral or IV 500 mg twice daily`,`5 days`,`CYP3A4 inhibitor; pause statins`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`IV or IM 1-2 g once daily; child 50-100 mg/kg`,`Until oral switch, day 2-3`,`Biliary sludge; under 2% penicillin cross-allergy`],[`Levofloxacin (reserve)`,`Oral or IV 750 mg once daily`,`5 days`,`Masks TB; tendon rupture, QT prolongation`],[`Oseltamivir`,`Oral 75 mg twice daily (30 mg if CrCl 30-60)`,`5 days`,`Nausea; give if influenza positive`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`IV 200 mg a day`,`4-7 days, then taper`,`Severe CAP only; hyperglycaemia; not in influenza`]]},{heading:`CURB-65 - one point each`,columns:[`Letter`,`Criterion`],rows:[[`C - Confusion`,`New disorientation, or AMT 8 or less`],[`U - Urea`,`Over 7 mmol/L (about 42 mg/dL urea)`],[`R - Respiratory rate`,`30 per minute or more`],[`B - Blood pressure`,`Systolic under 90 or diastolic 60 or less`],[`65 - Age`,`65 years or more`]]},{heading:`Childhood pneumonia, 2-59 months (SAANS)`,columns:[`Class`,`Signs`,`Action`],rows:[[`No pneumonia`,`No fast breathing, no indrawing, no danger sign`,`Home care, no antibiotic`],[`Pneumonia`,`Fast breathing and/or chest indrawing`,`Oral amoxicillin 5 days, review day 3`],[`Severe pneumonia`,`Danger sign, stridor when calm, or SpO2 under 90%`,`Oxygen, first IM dose, urgent referral`]]},{heading:`Fast breathing cut-offs in children`,columns:[`Age`,`Fast breathing`],rows:[[`Under 2 months`,`60 or more per minute`],[`2-11 months`,`50 or more per minute`],[`1-5 years`,`40 or more per minute`]]}],remember:[`CURB-65: Confusion, Urea over 7, RR 30 or more, BP low, age 65 or more.`,`CURB-65 0-1 home, 2 hospital-supervised, 3-5 admit, 4-5 think ICU.`,`SpO2 beats the score: under 92% means admission even at CURB-65 of 0.`,`Amoxicillin first; add a macrolide or doxycycline, never a macrolide alone.`,`Five days is enough for most CAP.`,`Fluoroquinolones mask TB - send sputum NAAT first.`,`Childhood pneumonia is counted, not heard: count RR for a full minute.`]},updated:[`ATS 2025 CAP update: systemic corticosteroids suggested in severe CAP (not influenza) and strongly recommended against in non-severe CAP`,`ATS 2025: under 5 days of antibiotics (e.g. 3 days) reasonable in non-severe CAP once clinically stable; lung ultrasound accepted as an alternative to chest X-ray`,`Surviving Sepsis 2026: antibiotics within 1 h in shock or probable sepsis, within 3 h if sepsis only possible without shock; at least 30 mL/kg crystalloid within 3 h; MAP 65 mmHg (60-65 if 65 years and over)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Community-acquired pneumonia`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute lower respiratory infection acquired outside hospital (or under 48 h of admission) **with new radiographic shadowing** - no new shadow, no pneumonia.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**CURB-65**: Confusion, Urea over 7 mmol/L, RR 30+, SBP under 90 or DBP 60 or less, age 65+ -> **0-1 home, 2 hospital, 3-5 admit (4-5 ICU)**; **CRB-65** in clinic; **IDSA/ATS severe CAP** - 1 major or 3 minor; child **SAANS: fast breathing/indrawing = pneumonia, danger sign or SpO2 under 90% = severe**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**S. pneumoniae** commonest, H. influenzae, S. aureus (post-influenza), atypicals (Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila, Legionella), viruses; Indian mimics - **TB, melioidosis, scrub typhus, leptospirosis**; risks - age, smoking, alcohol, COPD, diabetes, aspiration, asplenia, HIV.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Microaspiration/inhalation -> alveolar macrophage TLRs -> **IL-1, TNF, IL-6, IL-8** -> neutrophil exudate -> **consolidation** (congestion -> red -> grey hepatisation -> resolution) -> intrapulmonary shunt -> **hypoxaemia**; spill-over -> sepsis (the C, U, R, B of CURB).`],[`Key history`,`Cough, fever, breathlessness, **pleuritic pain**, sputum type (rusty, red-currant jelly, foul); **elderly - confusion or falls only**; recent influenza; comorbidity; antibiotics in 3 months; aspiration risk; **TB features**; exposure (paddy, monsoon, eschar); allergy; home support.`],[`Key examination`,`**RR (full minute), pulse, BP, temperature, SpO2, AMT**; dullness, **bronchial breathing, crackles, increased vocal resonance**; stony dullness = effusion; sepsis signs; organism clues (herpes labialis, eschar, bullous myringitis); child - indrawing, danger signs.`],[`Investigations`,`**CXR PA** (or lung ultrasound), **SpO2 on air (under 92% = admit)**, urea/creatinine, CBC, glucose; **CRP under 20 no antibiotic, 20-100 delayed, over 100 give**; **sputum NAAT** if TB features; admitted moderate-severe: blood cultures, urinary antigens, ABG, lactate, HIV; flu/COVID test in season.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Within **4 h** (1 h in sepsis). **Low severity: amoxicillin 500 mg-1 g TDS x 5 days**; allergy - doxycycline 200 mg then 100 mg or clarithromycin 500 mg BD; **comorbid outpatient: amox-clav 625 TDS + azithromycin 500 mg x 3 days**; **never macrolide monotherapy in India**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**CURB 2**: amoxicillin 1 g TDS (or amox-clav 1.2 g IV) + clarithromycin/azithromycin. **CURB 3-5**: **ceftriaxone 2 g IV OD + azithromycin 500 mg IV**; Pseudomonas risk - piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g q6h; MRSA risk - vancomycin or linezolid; **hydrocortisone 200 mg/day x 4-7 days in severe CAP** (not influenza).`],[`Treatment - duration & IV-to-oral switch`,`**5 days** if afebrile 48 h and no more than 1 instability sign; **ATS 2025: under 5 days (e.g. 3) acceptable in stable non-severe CAP**; longer for S. aureus, Pseudomonas, Legionella, empyema; IV to oral by day 2-3 when stable.`],[`Special groups`,`**Child 2-59 months: amoxicillin 40-45 mg/kg/dose BD x 5 days**; severe - ampicillin 50 mg/kg + gentamicin 7.5 mg/kg; **pregnancy - amoxicillin, ceftriaxone, azithromycin; no doxycycline/FQ**; elderly - atypical presentation; CKD - reduce amoxicillin, clarithromycin, levofloxacin, oseltamivir.`],[`Complications`,`Parapneumonic effusion/**empyema (pH under 7.2 -> drain)**, lung abscess, sepsis/ARDS, bacteraemic seeding (meningitis, endocarditis), **cardiac events (MI, AF) in up to a quarter**, non-resolution.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**CRB-65 3+, SpO2 under 92%**, RR 30+, SBP under 90, lactate over 2, new confusion, multilobar, effusion, cavitation after influenza (S. aureus), cough over 2 weeks (TB), no improvement at 48-72 h.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Review at 48-72 h**; recovery - fever 1 week, cough 6 weeks, fatigue 3-6 months; **repeat CXR at 6 weeks** only if symptoms persist or smoker/over 50; non-resolving - wrong organism (TB), complication, obstruction, wrong diagnosis.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**PCV in UIP (6 wk, 14 wk, 9 mo booster)**; adults 65+/comorbid - PCV20 or PCV13/15 then PPSV23; annual influenza; COVID-19; smoking cessation; swallow assessment after stroke; **SAANS** campaign for childhood pneumonia.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ATS 2025**: steroids only in severe CAP (not influenza), strongly against in non-severe; under 5 days if stable; lung ultrasound accepted. **SSC 2026**: antibiotics 1 h (shock/probable) or 3 h (possible), MAP 60-65 in 65+.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**SpO2 beats the score**; amoxicillin first because pneumococcus kills; **fluoroquinolones reserved in India - they mask TB**; macrolide resistance over 30%; childhood pneumonia is counted, not heard - **50 at 2-11 months, 40 at 1-5 years**.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`CAP in the clinic - severity to site of care`,steps:[{label:`Cough, fever, breathlessness with focal signs`,detail:`CXR (or lung ultrasound) to confirm new shadowing`},{label:`Vitals + SpO2 on room air`,detail:`SpO2 under 92% (88% COPD, 90% child) = admit whatever the score`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Score CRB-65 / CURB-65`,detail:`Confusion, urea over 7, RR 30+, SBP under 90/DBP 60 or less, age 65+`,tone:`decision`},{label:`0-1: home, amoxicillin 5 days`,detail:`500 mg-1 g TDS; add macrolide/doxycycline if comorbid or atypical`},{label:`2: hospital-supervised`,detail:`Amoxicillin 1 g TDS or amox-clav IV + macrolide`},{label:`3-5: admit, consider ICU at 4-5`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 2 g + azithromycin; hydrocortisone 200 mg/day if severe (not flu)`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Review 48-72 h; stop at 5 days (under 5 if stable)`,detail:`Not improving - TB NAAT, effusion, abscess, obstruction`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Empirical antibiotic ladder for adult CAP`,steps:[{label:`Low severity, no comorbidity`,detail:`Amoxicillin 500 mg-1 g TDS x 5 days; allergy - doxycycline or clarithromycin`},{label:`Outpatient with comorbidity`,detail:`Amox-clav 625 mg TDS + azithromycin 500 mg OD x 3 days (or doxycycline)`},{label:`Moderate (CURB 2), admitted`,detail:`Amoxicillin 1 g TDS or amox-clav 1.2 g IV q8h + clarithromycin 500 mg BD`},{label:`Severe (CURB 3-5) or ICU`,detail:`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV OD + azithromycin 500 mg IV; steroid if severe (ATS 2025)`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Specific risks only`,detail:`Pseudomonas - pip-taz 4.5 g q6h; MRSA - vancomycin or linezolid; flu - oseltamivir 75 mg BD`,tone:`decision`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Pneumonia with sepsis - first hour (SSC 2026)`,steps:[{label:`Suspected infection + organ dysfunction`,detail:`Hypotension, confusion, lactate over 2, low urine output`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Blood cultures, then antibiotics`,detail:`Within 1 h if shock or probable sepsis; within 3 h if only possible and no shock`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Crystalloid at least 30 mL/kg within 3 h`,detail:`For hypoperfusion; reassess often to avoid overload`},{label:`Noradrenaline if still hypotensive`,detail:`MAP 65 mmHg (60-65 in 65 years and over)`},{label:`Hydrocortisone 200 mg/day IV`,detail:`Septic shock or severe CAP`},{label:`ICU, oxygen, HFNO/NIV as needed`,tone:`good`}]}]},"respiratory-acute-cough-influenza-covid":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Acute cough** lasts under 3 weeks; subacute 3-8 weeks; chronic over 8 weeks.`,`In India, **cough of 2 weeks or more is presumptive TB** and needs sputum NAAT.`,`Over 90% of acute cough in adults is viral and **needs no antibiotic**.`,`**Acute bronchitis** is cough for 1-3 weeks with no pneumonia on examination or X-ray.`,`**ILI** is measured fever of 38 C or more with cough, onset within 10 days.`,`**SARI** is the same illness needing hospital admission; both are reported to IDSP.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Influenza **Category A**: mild illness; no test and no oseltamivir.`,`**Category B**: high fever and severe sore throat, or any high-risk person; oseltamivir without testing.`,`**Category C**: breathlessness, chest pain, drowsiness or low BP; test, admit, treat at once.`,`COVID **mild**: no breathlessness and **SpO2 94% or more** on air.`,`COVID **moderate**: RR 24-30 or SpO2 90-93%; **severe**: RR over 30 or SpO2 under 90%.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Viruses strip ciliated epithelium and sensitise cough nerve endings, so cough outlasts the fever.`,`Green sputum colour comes from **neutrophil myeloperoxidase**, not from bacteria.`,`Influenza **neuraminidase** releases new virions; **oseltamivir blocks it** and stops spread.`,`**Antigenic drift** causes yearly epidemics; **antigenic shift** causes pandemics.`,`SARS-CoV-2 enters cells through **ACE2**, after spike cleavage by TMPRSS2.`,`**Silent hypoxaemia**: COVID lowers SpO2 while CO2 and lung stiffness stay normal.`,`Pertussis toxin raises cAMP, causing **marked lymphocytosis**; ciliary damage prolongs cough.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask the **duration in days**; 2 weeks or more triggers a TB sputum NAAT.`,`**Abrupt fever, myalgia and prostration** suggest influenza; loss of smell suggests COVID-19.`,`Paroxysms ending in a **whoop or vomiting**, well between bouts, suggest pertussis.`,`Breathlessness, rigors or pleuritic pain point to pneumonia or pulmonary embolism.`,`Identify **high-risk groups**: pregnancy, under 5, 65 and over, chronic disease, immunosuppression.`,`Ask about ACE inhibitors, cough syrups given to children, and smoke exposure.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **temperature, pulse, RR, BP and SpO2** in every patient with acute cough.`,`**Pulse over 100, RR over 24, fever over 38 C**, focal signs or SpO2 under 94% suggest pneumonia.`,`With none of these discriminators, pneumonia is unlikely and no X-ray is needed.`,`In COVID, a **fall of 3% or more on the 6-minute walk** means hidden hypoxaemia.`,`In a child look for stridor (croup), unilateral wheeze (foreign body) and petechiae (pertussis).`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Uncomplicated acute cough needs no test**; diagnosis is clinical.`,`Chest X-ray only with a discriminator, cough over 3 weeks, or a smoker over 40.`,`**CRP**: under 20 no antibiotic, 20-100 delayed prescription, over 100 antibiotic.`,`Influenza RT-PCR only in **Category C**; a negative rapid test never withholds oseltamivir.`,`COVID rapid antigen misses cases; confirm a negative in a suspected case with **RT-PCR**.`,`Pertussis: **nasopharyngeal PCR** within the first 3 weeks of cough.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Tell patients bronchitis cough lasts **about 18 days**; antibiotics shorten it by half a day.`,`Advise rest, warm fluids, **paracetamol**, and stopping smoking.`,`**Honey 2.5-5 mL at bedtime** for night cough, but **never under 1 year**.`,`**No cough syrups under 2 years**, and generally none under 5 years.`,`**Never give aspirin to children** with influenza; it causes Reye syndrome.`,`Salbutamol only if there is **wheeze or known asthma or COPD**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days** for Category B and C, without waiting for a test.`,`Oseltamivir is best within 48 hours, but give it **at any stage** if severe or high-risk.`,`Prophylaxis: the same dose **once daily for 10 days** in high-risk unvaccinated contacts.`,`Mild COVID: **no steroid, no antibiotic**, no ivermectin or vitamin cocktails.`,`**Dexamethasone 6 mg daily for 5-10 days only if the patient needs oxygen**.`,`High-risk mild COVID: **nirmatrelvir-ritonavir** within 5 days of onset; check CYP3A4 interactions.`,`Pertussis: **azithromycin 5 days** for the patient and the whole household.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Fever returning after improvement suggests **secondary bacterial pneumonia** after influenza.`,`Necrotising post-influenza pneumonia suggests **S. aureus**; add vancomycin or linezolid.`,`**COVID with SpO2 under 94%** needs admission, oxygen to 92-96% and awake proning.`,`An **infant with pertussis** and apnoea or cyanosis needs admission.`,`Diabetic after COVID with nasal black eschar or proptosis: suspect **mucormycosis**, refer today.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**Annual influenza vaccine** for high-risk groups, health workers and **pregnant women in any trimester**.`,`India uses the **Southern Hemisphere vaccine in April-May**, before the monsoon peak.`,`Children 6 months-8 years vaccinated for the first time need **two doses 4 weeks apart**.`,`Egg allergy is not a contraindication to influenza vaccine.`,`**Tdap in every pregnancy at 27-36 weeks** protects the newborn from pertussis.`,`Report ILI, SARI and **clusters the same day** to IDSP through the IHIP portal.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Acute cough, influenza and COVID drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Paracetamol`,`Oral 500 mg-1 g 6-hourly (max 4 g/day); child 15 mg/kg`,`While symptomatic`,`Liver toxicity in overdose; never aspirin in children`],[`Honey`,`2.5-5 mL at bedtime`,`Few days`,`Never under 1 year (botulism)`],[`Oseltamivir`,`Oral 75 mg BD; child 30-75 mg by weight; infant 3 mg/kg`,`5 days (prophylaxis OD 10 days)`,`Nausea; reduce dose if CrCl under 60`],[`Amoxicillin (only if indicated)`,`Oral 500 mg three times daily`,`5 days`,`Only for pneumonia or CRP over 100`],[`Azithromycin (pertussis)`,`Adult 500 mg then 250 mg; infant 10 mg/kg daily`,`5 days`,`QT prolongation; preferred over erythromycin in infants`],[`Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir`,`Oral 300/100 mg BD (150/100 if eGFR 30-60)`,`5 days, start within 5 days`,`Hold statins, colchicine, rivaroxaban`],[`Remdesivir`,`IV 200 mg day 1, then 100 mg daily`,`5 days total (3 in outpatients)`,`Transaminase rise, bradycardia`],[`Dexamethasone`,`Oral or IV 6 mg once daily`,`5-10 days, only on oxygen`,`Hyperglycaemia, mucormycosis risk`],[`Enoxaparin`,`SC 40 mg once daily`,`During admission`,`Bleeding; adjust if CrCl under 30`]]},{heading:`MoHFW influenza categories`,columns:[`Category`,`Features`,`Test`,`Oseltamivir and setting`],rows:[[`A`,`Mild fever, cough, sore throat, body ache`,`Not needed`,`No; home care, review 24-48 h`],[`B (i)`,`A plus high fever, severe sore throat`,`Not needed`,`Yes; home isolation`],[`B (ii)`,`A in a high-risk person`,`Not needed`,`Yes, without waiting; close follow-up`],[`C`,`Breathlessness, chest pain, drowsiness, low BP, cyanosis`,`RT-PCR swab`,`Yes, immediately; admit`]]},{heading:`COVID-19 severity (AIIMS-ICMR)`,columns:[`Severity`,`Criteria`,`Setting`,`Key treatment`],rows:[[`Mild`,`No breathlessness, SpO2 94% or more`,`Home with oximeter`,`Symptomatic; no steroid, no antibiotic`],[`Moderate`,`RR 24-30 or SpO2 90-93%`,`Ward with oxygen`,`Oxygen, proning, dexamethasone, enoxaparin`],[`Severe`,`RR over 30 or SpO2 under 90%`,`HDU or ICU`,`HFNO or NIV, steroid, tocilizumab if progressing`]]}],remember:[`Acute cough is under 3 weeks, but 2 weeks makes it presumptive TB in India.`,`Purulent sputum is neutrophils, not bacteria.`,`Category B(ii) - high-risk person with ILI gets oseltamivir without a test.`,`Oseltamivir 75 mg BD for 5 days; prophylaxis OD for 10 days.`,`In COVID the steroid is only for the patient on oxygen.`,`Pertussis antibiotics stop transmission, not the cough - treat the household.`,`No cough syrups under 2 years; honey only after 1 year.`]},updated:[`WHO 2024 influenza clinical guideline added: baloxavir (single dose) suggested for non-severe influenza at high risk, strong recommendation against oseltamivir in non-severe low-risk influenza, oseltamivir for severe influenza, no adjunct steroids/macrolides/NSAIDs`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Acute cough, ILI and COVID-19`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Acute cough under 3 weeks**, subacute 3-8, chronic over 8 weeks; **acute bronchitis** = cough 1-3 weeks without pneumonia; **ILI** = fever 38 C+ and cough within 10 days; **SARI** = ILI needing admission; in India **cough 2+ weeks = presumptive TB**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**MoHFW influenza: A** (mild - no test, no oseltamivir), **B** (high fever/severe sore throat or high-risk - oseltamivir without test), **C** (breathlessness, chest pain, drowsiness, low BP - test, admit, oseltamivir). **COVID (AIIMS/ICMR)**: mild SpO2 94%+, moderate RR 24-30 or SpO2 90-93%, severe RR over 30 or SpO2 under 90%.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Rhinovirus, influenza, SARS-CoV-2, RSV; pertussis; pneumonia; asthma/COPD flare; irritant smoke; ACE inhibitors; mimics - **heart failure, PE, foreign body**. High-risk for flu/COVID: **pregnancy, under 5, 65+, chronic disease, obesity, immunosuppression**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Virus sheds ciliated epithelium -> exposed C-fibres (TRPV1) -> **cough hypersensitivity** (weeks); **purulent sputum = neutrophil myeloperoxidase, not bacteria**; influenza **neuraminidase** releases virions (oseltamivir target); SARS-CoV-2 **ACE2/TMPRSS2** -> shunt -> **silent hypoxaemia**; pertussis toxin -> lymphocytosis.`],[`Key history`,`**Duration in days**; abrupt fever and myalgia (flu), anosmia (COVID), whoop/post-tussive vomit (pertussis); breathlessness, pleuritic pain (pneumonia, PE); asthma/COPD; high-risk status; contacts/clusters; vaccines; drugs (ACEi, nirmatrelvir interactions); antibiotic expectation.`],[`Key examination`,`**Pulse, RR, temperature, SpO2** - any of pulse over 100, RR over 24, T over 38, focal signs, SpO2 under 94% -> CXR; **walk test or sit-to-stand** in COVID (fall 3%+ = hypoxaemia); child - subconjunctival haemorrhage (pertussis), stridor, unilateral wheeze.`],[`Investigations`,`**Uncomplicated cough - no tests**; CXR only with discriminators; **CRP under 20 no antibiotic, 20-100 delayed, over 100 antibiotic**; **sputum NAAT at 2 weeks**; influenza RT-PCR only in Category C/admitted; COVID RAT then RT-PCR; pertussis PCR in first 3 weeks.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Explain **cough lasts ~18 days**; fluids, honey (over 1 year), paracetamol; **no antibiotic** for cold, bronchitis, ILI; **no cough syrups under 2 years**; **oseltamivir 75 mg BD x 5 days for Category B and C without waiting for a test** (child by weight 30/45/60/75 mg).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Antibiotic only for pneumonia, pertussis, purulent COPD exacerbation, frail/immunocompromised, CRP over 100 - **amoxicillin 500 mg TDS x 5 days**; **WHO 2024: baloxavir single dose for non-severe high-risk flu (where available)**; COVID high-risk mild - **nirmatrelvir-ritonavir 300/100 BD x 5 days**; on oxygen - **dexamethasone 6 mg x 5-10 days**, remdesivir, enoxaparin; progressing - tocilizumab or baricitinib.`],[`Treatment - pertussis`,`**Azithromycin - under 6 months 10 mg/kg OD x 5 days; older 10 mg/kg then 5 mg/kg x 4 days; adult 500 then 250 mg**; give within 3 weeks of cough; treat household; exclude from school till 5 days of treatment; **Tdap at 27-36 weeks in every pregnancy**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy: oseltamivir same day**, paracetamol, azithromycin, enoxaparin safe; prednisolone instead of dexamethasone (COVID) unless fetal lung maturation needed; **children: no aspirin (Reye), no codeine under 12**, honey not under 1 year; CKD - oseltamivir 30 mg BD (CrCl 30-60).`],[`Complications`,`Secondary bacterial pneumonia (**S. aureus after influenza**), ARDS, myocarditis, **post-COVID mucormycosis** in steroid-treated diabetics, post-COVID condition (beyond 12 weeks), infant pertussis apnoea and pulmonary hypertension.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`SpO2 under 94%, RR over 24, chest pain, drowsiness, haemoptysis (**Category C**); pregnant woman with ILI and breathlessness; infant with paroxysmal cough/apnoea; **cough 2+ weeks (TB)**; black nasal eschar or proptosis (mucormycosis).`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review if worsening or not settling by 3 weeks; high-risk flu - phone at 24-48 h; COVID home - SpO2 twice daily, isolation ends 7 days with 3 afebrile days; post-COVID pneumonia - CXR and spirometry at 12 weeks.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Annual influenza vaccine (Southern Hemisphere formulation, April-May)** for high-risk, health workers, pregnant women any trimester; 2 doses 4 weeks apart in first-time children 6 months-8 years; UIP pentavalent 6-10-14 weeks + DPT boosters; **IDSP S/P/L forms**, same-day cluster reporting; AMR stewardship.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO 2024 influenza guideline**: baloxavir for non-severe high-risk, oseltamivir for severe, against oseltamivir in non-severe low-risk; India still triages oseltamivir by **MoHFW A/B/C**; MoHFW October 2025 - no cough syrups under 2 years.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Category B(ii) gets oseltamivir without a test**; prophylaxis = same dose once daily x 10 days; **in COVID the steroid is for the patient on oxygen**; azithromycin not erythromycin in neonates (pyloric stenosis); pertussis antibiotics stop spread, not the cough.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute cough in the OPD - who needs what`,steps:[{label:`Cough under 3 weeks`,detail:`If 2+ weeks or TB symptoms - sputum NAAT now`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Any discriminator?`,detail:`Pulse over 100, RR over 24, T over 38 C, focal signs, SpO2 under 94%`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - CXR, treat as pneumonia if shadow`,detail:`Amoxicillin 500 mg TDS x 5 days; CRP if uncertain`,tone:`warn`},{label:`ILI - apply MoHFW A / B / C`,detail:`B and C get oseltamivir 75 mg BD x 5 days without waiting for a test`},{label:`Suspected COVID - oximetry and walk test`,detail:`Fall 3%+ or SpO2 under 94% = admit; high-risk mild - nirmatrelvir-ritonavir`},{label:`Bronchitis or cold - no antibiotic`,detail:`Explain ~18-day course, honey, paracetamol, safety-net advice`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Influenza versus COVID-19 versus pertussis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Influenza`,`COVID-19`,`Pertussis`],rows:[[`Onset`,`Abrupt fever, myalgia, prostration`,`Fever, anosmia, sore throat; silent hypoxaemia`,`Cold then paroxysms, whoop, post-tussive vomit`],[`Test`,`RT-PCR only in Category C/admitted`,`RAT then RT-PCR if negative`,`Nasopharyngeal PCR in first 3 weeks; lymphocytosis`],[`Specific drug`,`Oseltamivir 75 mg BD x 5 days (baloxavir per WHO 2024)`,`Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir (high-risk mild); dexamethasone only on oxygen`,`Azithromycin 5 days (10 mg/kg then 5 mg/kg)`],[`Danger`,`Secondary S. aureus pneumonia; pregnancy`,`Hypoxaemia, thrombosis, mucormycosis after steroids`,`Apnoea and death in infants under 6 months`],[`Prevention`,`Annual vaccine April-May, any trimester`,`Vaccination per policy, isolation 7 days`,`Tdap 27-36 weeks; DPT boosters; household prophylaxis`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`COVID-19 treatment by severity (AIIMS/ICMR)`,steps:[{label:`Mild (SpO2 94%+): home isolation`,detail:`Paracetamol, fluids, oximetry; no steroid, no antibiotic, no ivermectin/HCQ`},{label:`Mild but high risk, within 5 days`,detail:`Nirmatrelvir-ritonavir 300/100 mg BD x 5 days (check CYP3A4 interactions)`},{label:`Moderate (SpO2 90-93%, RR 24-30)`,detail:`Oxygen 92-96%, awake proning, dexamethasone 6 mg, enoxaparin 40 mg, remdesivir within 10 days`},{label:`Severe (SpO2 under 90%, RR over 30)`,detail:`HFNO/NIV/ventilation; tocilizumab 8 mg/kg or baricitinib 4 mg if progressing`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"respiratory-tb-infection-contacts-tpt":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**TB infection** is a persistent immune response to M. tuberculosis without active disease.`,`The person is **asymptomatic and not infectious**; WHO no longer says 'latent TB'.`,`About **30-40% of Indian adults** carry TB infection, the reservoir of future cases.`,`TPT cuts progression to disease by **60-90%** in those who complete it.`,`A **household contact** shared the home for a night or more in the 3 months before treatment.`,`For a child with TB, **reverse contact tracing** finds the adult source.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**All PLHIV** are eligible for TPT once active TB is excluded.`,`**Household contacts under 5 years** get TPT with no test of infection.`,`Household contacts **5 years and over** get TPT, after a test of infection where available.`,`Other risk groups (anti-TNF, dialysis, transplant, silicosis) need a positive test first.`,`The **index case resistance pattern** decides the regimen for every contact.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Alveolar macrophages ingest inhaled bacilli that innate immunity fails to clear.`,`Th1 cells release **interferon-gamma**, and a granuloma holds the bacilli dormant.`,`Lifetime risk of disease is **5-10%, half within the first 2 years**.`,`Infants progress most: **40-50% under 1 year**, often to miliary or meningeal TB.`,`**HIV** raises the risk to about **10% per year**.`,`Infection holds few bacilli, so one or two drugs suffice without breeding resistance.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Find the **index case's drug resistance pattern** and treatment start date.`,`Ask the **four-symptom screen**: cough of any duration, fever, weight loss, night sweats.`,`**Any positive symptom** makes the contact presumptive TB, not a TPT candidate.`,`In children ask about poor weight gain, lethargy and reduced playfulness.`,`Ask about HIV, liver disease, alcohol, ART regimen and pregnancy.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record weight and plot children on a **growth chart**; faltering growth is a TB symptom.`,`Fever or tachypnoea in a contact means evaluation for disease, not TPT.`,`Examine all **lymph node groups**, the chest and the abdomen.`,`Look for jaundice before starting hepatotoxic drugs.`,`**Any abnormal finding** moves the contact to the active TB pathway.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Exclude active TB first**: four-symptom screen plus chest X-ray.`,`Any symptom or X-ray abnormality means **sputum NAAT** before any TPT.`,`**Mantoux**: 0.1 mL of 2 TU PPD RT23 intradermally, read at **48-72 hours**.`,`Measure the **transverse induration** in millimetres, not the redness.`,`**IGRA and Cy-Tb** use ESAT-6 and CFP-10, so BCG does not cause false positives.`,`**No test of infection is needed** for under-5 contacts or PLHIV.`,`Baseline LFT only for those at risk: liver disease, alcohol, HIV, pregnancy, elderly.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**3HP** for most adults and children 2 years and over: 12 weekly doses.`,`Adult 3HP: **isoniazid 900 mg plus rifapentine 900 mg once weekly**, with food.`,`**6H** where rifapentine cannot be used: under 2 years, pregnancy, PI or nevirapine ART.`,`6H dose: **5 mg/kg in adults, 10 mg/kg in children**, maximum 300 mg daily.`,`Give **pyridoxine 10-25 mg daily** with isoniazid in at-risk groups.`,`Supervise weekly doses on a **fixed weekday**, and line-list contacts on Ni-kshay.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**1HP**: isoniazid 300 mg plus rifapentine 600 mg daily for 28 days, age 13 and over.`,`**3HR** daily for 3 months suits young children who cannot take rifapentine.`,`**4R**: rifampicin daily for 4 months for isoniazid intolerance or Hr-TB index.`,`**MDR-TB contacts: levofloxacin daily for 6 months**, nationwide since August 2025.`,`Contacts of fluoroquinolone-resistant TB get 2-year clinical follow-up instead.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: use 6H with pyridoxine; avoid rifapentine.`,`**Neonate of a mother with TB**: 6H, then **BCG at the end of TPT**.`,`3HP works with **efavirenz and dolutegravir**, but not protease inhibitors or nevirapine.`,`Rifamycins make the pill fail; advise **condoms, copper IUCD or DMPA**.`,`BCG-vaccinated child contacts still need TPT.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review monthly on 6H, and at weeks 2, 4, 8 and 12 on 3HP.`,`Stop TPT if **ALT is over 3 times ULN with symptoms**, or over 5 times without.`,`A **flu-like reaction** hours after a weekly rifapentine dose means stop 3HP and review.`,`**Completion = 80% of doses within 133% of the planned time**.`,`Follow MDR-TB contacts with **screening every 6 months for 2 years**.`,`Any symptom during TPT means NAAT; if TB is confirmed, start full treatment.`]}],tables:[{heading:`TPT regimens at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`6H - isoniazid`,`Oral 5 mg/kg adult, 10 mg/kg child, max 300 mg`,`Daily for 6 months`,`Hepatitis, neuropathy; add pyridoxine`],[`3HP - isoniazid plus rifapentine`,`Adult over 30 kg: H 900 + P 900 mg weekly`,`12 weekly doses`,`Flu-like reaction; not in pregnancy or under 2`],[`3HR - isoniazid plus rifampicin`,`Child H 10 + R 15 mg/kg; adult H 5 + R 10 mg/kg`,`Daily for 3 months`,`Hepatitis, orange secretions`],[`1HP - isoniazid plus rifapentine`,`Oral H 300 + P 600 mg daily`,`28 days, age 13 and over`,`As 3HP; not in pregnancy`],[`4R - rifampicin`,`Oral 10 mg/kg, max 600 mg`,`Daily for 4 months`,`Hepatitis; check enzyme-inducer interactions`],[`6Lfx - levofloxacin`,`Adult 750 mg-1 g; child 15-20 mg/kg`,`Daily for 6 months`,`Tendinopathy, QT prolongation; MDR contacts only`],[`Pyridoxine`,`Oral 10-25 mg daily`,`Throughout isoniazid TPT`,`None at these doses`]]},{heading:`Mantoux (TST) cut-offs`,columns:[`Induration at 48-72 hours`,`Positive in`,`Note`],rows:[[`5 mm or more`,`PLHIV, severe malnutrition, immunosuppressed`,`Response is blunted`],[`10 mm or more`,`Everyone else, including contacts and health workers`,`BCG and NTM can give false positives`],[`Below cut-off`,`Window period, anergy, infant under 6 months`,`Does not exclude infection`]]},{heading:`Tests of infection compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Mantoux (TST)`,`IGRA`,`Cy-Tb`],rows:[[`Antigen`,`PPD (crude mixture)`,`ESAT-6 and CFP-10`,`ESAT-6 and CFP-10`],[`Affected by BCG`,`Yes`,`No`,`No`],[`Visits needed`,`Two`,`One`,`Two`],[`Positive at`,`5 or 10 mm`,`0.35 IU/mL or more`,`5 mm or more`],[`Diagnoses active TB`,`No`,`No`,`No`]]}],remember:[`Exclude active TB before TPT - symptoms plus chest X-ray, NAAT if either is abnormal.`,`No test of infection is needed for under-5 contacts or PLHIV.`,`3HP = 900 mg isoniazid + 900 mg rifapentine weekly for 12 weeks.`,`6H is the fallback for under-2s, pregnancy and PI or nevirapine ART.`,`MDR-TB contacts get 6 months of levofloxacin, plus 6-monthly screening for 2 years.`,`Completion = 80% of doses within 133% of planned time; add missed doses at the end.`,`Mantoux: transverse induration at 48-72 hours; 5 mm in PLHIV, 10 mm in others.`]},updated:[`Central TB Division DO letter (6 August 2025): nationwide scale-up of 3HP for contacts aged 2-14 and DS-TB household contacts, and of 6Lfx or 4R (by index resistance pattern) for eligible MDR/RR-TB contacts after the 12-state pilot`,`1HP (isoniazid 300 mg + rifapentine 600 mg daily x 28 days, 13 years and over) adopted by NTEP as an addendum to the PMTPT guidelines, December 2024; NACP uses 1HP for PLHIV since 2024`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - TB infection, contacts and TPT`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**TB infection (TBI)** = persistent immune response to M. tuberculosis antigens **without clinical, radiological or microbiological disease**; asymptomatic, not infectious; household contact = shared space 1+ night or frequent daytime in the **3 months before the index started treatment**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**TPT eligibility (NTEP)**: (1) **all PLHIV**; (2) **household contacts under 5 - no test needed**; (3) **contacts 5+** - after test of infection where available; (4) other risk groups (anti-TNF, dialysis, transplant, silicosis) - after positive test; DR-TB contacts by index resistance.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Recent household exposure to pulmonary TB; progression 5-10% lifetime, **half in 2 years**; **infants 40-50%**; **HIV ~10%/year**; undernutrition, diabetes (3x), **silicosis (30x)**, anti-TNF, dialysis, transplant, steroids.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Inhaled bacilli -> macrophages -> **Th1 IFN-gamma** -> granuloma holds few bacilli (well under 10^5) -> **1-2 drugs suffice** (no resistant mutant); disease has 10^9 -> monotherapy breeds resistance -> **exclude disease first**; rifapentine half-life 13-15 h allows weekly dosing.`],[`Key history`,`Index case - site, NAAT, **resistance pattern**, start date; exposure intensity; **four-symptom screen (cough any duration, fever, weight loss, night sweats)**; child - poor weight gain, lethargy; HIV, liver disease, alcohol; **ART regimen** and other interacting drugs; pregnancy.`],[`Key examination`,`Weight, BMI or growth chart; temperature, RR, SpO2; all node groups; chest; hepatosplenomegaly; TBM signs in infants; jaundice; read Mantoux **transverse induration at 48-72 h** - any abnormal finding moves the contact to the **active TB pathway**.`],[`Investigations`,`**Symptom screen + CXR** for contacts; **any abnormality -> NAAT** (gastric aspirate/induced sputum in young children); PLHIV - CRP over 5 mg/L, LF-LAM; **TST (2 TU PPD RT23): 5 mm+ in PLHIV/malnourished, 10 mm+ others**; IGRA 0.35 IU/mL+; **Cy-Tb 5 mm+**; LFT only if at risk.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**3HP - isoniazid 900 + rifapentine 900 mg weekly x 12** (adults over 30 kg; children 2-14 by weight), with food, observed; **6H - isoniazid 5 mg/kg (child 10 mg/kg) daily x 6 months** for under-2s, pregnancy, PI/nevirapine ART; pyridoxine 10-25 mg if at risk.`],[`Treatment - alternatives & DR-TB contacts`,`**1HP - H 300 + P 600 mg daily x 28 days (13 years+)**; **3HR** (child dispersible HR 50/75); **4R** rifampicin 10 mg/kg x 4 months (INH intolerance or Hr-TB index); **6Lfx** for MDR/RR-TB contacts (FQ-susceptible) - adult 750 mg (under 46 kg) or 1 g, child 15-20 mg/kg daily x 6 months - **scaled up nationally August 2025**.`],[`Drug adverse effects`,`INH - hepatitis (age over 35, alcohol, pregnancy), neuropathy; rifapentine - **flu-like/hypersensitivity reaction hours after weekly dose**, orange secretions, CYP3A4 induction; levofloxacin - tendinopathy, QT; **stop if ALT over 3x ULN with symptoms or 5x without**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy: 6H with pyridoxine**, no rifapentine; **neonate of TB mother - 6H then BCG**, continue breastfeeding; **PLHIV - 3HP with dolutegravir or efavirenz, not with PIs/nevirapine**; health workers - two-step TST, TPT after conversion.`],[`Complications`,`Progression to disease (especially **infants - miliary/meningeal TB**), DILI, rifapentine reactions, failed contraception, **resistance if active TB missed**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Any symptom or CXR abnormality in a contact (NAAT before TPT); **child under 5 not screened within a week**; jaundice on TPT; rifapentine hypotension/syncope; MDR contact with symptoms; complex ART or transplant interactions.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Monthly on 6H; weeks 2, 4, 8, 12 on 3HP; **completion = 80% of doses within 133% of time** (6H ~146 of 182 doses in ~8 months; 3HP 11 of 12 doses in 16 weeks); outcomes on Ni-kshay; **household follow-up 2 years**; MDR contacts screened 6-monthly x 2 years.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**PMTPT 2021** extended TPT to contacts of all ages; **CTD 2025 scale-up of 3HP and 6Lfx/4R**; line-list contacts on Ni-kshay at notification; Ni-kshay Mitra nutrition (RATIONS - 40-50% fewer cases); BCG at birth.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Aug 2025 CTD**: 3HP for paediatric and DS-TB contacts, **6Lfx/4R for MDR/RR-TB contacts in all states**; **Dec 2024 NTEP addendum: 1HP from 13 years**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Exclude active TB before TPT**; no test needed under 5 or in PLHIV; **IGRA/Cy-Tb (ESAT-6, CFP-10, RD1) unaffected by BCG**; 3HP from age 2, not in pregnancy; rifamycins make OCPs fail.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Household contact - screen, exclude, protect`,steps:[{label:`Index pulmonary TB notified`,detail:`Line-list household contacts on Ni-kshay; note index resistance`},{label:`Four-symptom screen + chest X-ray`,detail:`Cough any duration, fever, weight loss, night sweats`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Any symptom or CXR abnormality`,detail:`Presumptive TB - NAAT, never TPT`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Test of infection?`,detail:`Not needed under 5 or in PLHIV; TST/IGRA/Cy-Tb for 5+ where available`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Pick regimen by index resistance and patient`,detail:`DS index - 3HP / 6H / 1HP (13+); MDR/RR index - 6Lfx or 4R`},{label:`Complete and record outcome`,detail:`80% doses within 133% of time; follow household 2 years`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`TPT regimens - who gets which`,columns:[`Regimen`,`Dose`,`Use in`,`Avoid in`],rows:[[`3HP (weekly x 12)`,`H 900 + P 900 mg (adult over 30 kg); child by weight`,`Most contacts 2 years+, PLHIV on DTG/EFV`,`Pregnancy, under 2, PI or nevirapine ART`],[`1HP (daily x 28)`,`H 300 + P 600 mg`,`13 years and over (NTEP addendum 2024)`,`Pregnancy`],[`6H (daily x 6 months)`,`5 mg/kg adult (300 mg), 10 mg/kg child`,`Under 2, pregnancy, PI/nevirapine ART, neonates`,`Active hepatitis, heavy alcohol (relative)`],[`4R (daily x 4 months)`,`Rifampicin 10 mg/kg (max 600 mg)`,`INH intolerance; Hr-TB index`,`Interacting drugs (PIs, OCP, warfarin)`],[`6Lfx (daily x 6 months)`,`Adult 750 mg (under 46 kg) or 1 g; child 15-20 mg/kg`,`MDR/RR-TB contacts, FQ-susceptible index (national from Aug 2025)`,`FQ-resistant index - follow up 2 years instead`]]}]},"respiratory-pleural-effusion":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **pleural effusion** is any abnormal collection of fluid in the pleural space.`,`The normal space holds only about **10-20 mL** of lubricating fluid.`,`In India, **tuberculosis** is the commonest cause of an exudate.`,`**Heart failure** is the commonest cause of an effusion overall.`,`A massive effusion filling the hemithorax is **malignant until proved otherwise**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Light's criteria** sort fluid into transudate or exudate; any one criterion makes an exudate.`,`**Transudates**: heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome and hypoalbuminaemia.`,`**Exudates**: TB, parapneumonic effusion, malignancy, pulmonary embolism and connective tissue disease.`,`**Uncomplicated parapneumonic**: free-flowing, pH 7.2 or more; antibiotics alone.`,`**Complicated parapneumonic**: pH under 7.2, glucose under 60 mg/dL, or loculation; drain it.`,`**Empyema** is frank pus in the pleural space, and always needs a drain.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Fluid filters from parietal pleural capillaries and drains through **parietal lymphatic stomata**.`,`Effusion forms when filtration rises, oncotic pressure falls, or lymphatic drainage is blocked.`,`Hepatic hydrothorax is **right-sided in about 85%**, as ascites crosses diaphragmatic defects.`,`**TB effusion** is a Th1 hypersensitivity reaction, so the fluid is lymphocytic and paucibacillary.`,`Activated T cells release **ADA**, which is why ADA over 40 U/L suggests TB.`,`In infection, bacteria consume glucose and make acid, so **low pH and glucose** mean active infection.`,`Fibrin septa then wall off pockets that antibiotics alone cannot sterilise.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Pleuritic pain that **eases as breathlessness worsens** means the effusion is enlarging.`,`Evening fever, night sweats and weight loss over weeks suggest **TB pleurisy** in a young adult.`,`Fever persisting despite antibiotics after pneumonia suggests **empyema**.`,`Smoker over 50 with weight loss, haemoptysis or hoarseness suggests **malignancy**.`,`Ask about orthopnoea, ascites and frothy urine for the transudate causes.`,`Before a tap, ask about **anticoagulants and bleeding tendency**.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Stony dull percussion** rising into the axilla is the hallmark sign.`,`**Reduced breath sounds, fremitus and vocal resonance** over the fluid.`,`Bronchial breathing and aegophony appear just above the fluid level.`,`A large effusion pushes the **trachea away** from the affected side.`,`Massive effusion with a central trachea suggests **underlying lung collapse** from tumour.`,`Signs need about 300-500 mL of fluid, so imaging decides small effusions.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Chest X-ray PA** blunts the costophrenic angle at about **200 mL**.`,`**Thoracic ultrasound** detects 5-20 mL, shows septations and **must guide every tap**.`,`Tap every unilateral or unexplained effusion, but **not bilateral heart-failure effusions**.`,`Send **protein and LDH with paired serum**, glucose, pH, cell count, Gram stain, culture and cytology.`,`In India, send **ADA, Xpert Ultra and mycobacterial culture** on every lymphocytic exudate.`,`Contrast CT for an unexplained exudate; then **pleural biopsy**, not repeated taps.`]},{title:`Diagnosis`,points:[`**ADA over 40 U/L with lymphocytes over 50%** in a young adult means TB.`,`Mesothelial cells **under 5%** support a TB effusion.`,`Diuretic-treated heart failure can look like an exudate; an **albumin gradient over 1.2 g/dL** corrects it.`,`**Pleural NT-proBNP over 1500 pg/mL** also points to heart failure.`,`Bloody exudate in a smoker over 50 needs cytology and CT before empirical ATT.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Treat the cause**: diuretics for heart failure, ATT for TB, drainage for infection.`,`Heart failure effusion: **furosemide 40 mg** once daily in the morning.`,`TB pleurisy: **2HRZE + 4HRE for 6 months** by weight band; notify on Ni-kshay.`,`**Steroids are not routinely recommended** in TB pleurisy.`,`Empyema: **amoxicillin-clavulanate 1.2 g IV 8-hourly**, or ceftriaxone plus metronidazole.`,`**Avoid aminoglycosides** in empyema; they penetrate poorly and fail in acidic pus.`,`Give paracetamol and NSAIDs for pleuritic pain, so the patient can breathe deeply.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Use an **ultrasound-guided 12-14 French drain** for empyema and complicated effusion.`,`Insert drains in the **safe triangle**, above the upper border of the rib below.`,`Remove **no more than 1-1.5 L** at one therapeutic tap.`,`Not responding at 24-72 hours: **intrapleural tPA 10 mg plus DNase 5 mg** twice daily for 3 days.`,`Recurrent malignant effusion: **4 g talc pleurodesis**, or an indwelling pleural catheter.`,`Persistent sepsis or trapped lung: refer for **VATS decortication**.`,`**Never put a chest drain into a hepatic hydrothorax**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Stop a tap at once for chest tightness or cough: **re-expansion pulmonary oedema**.`,`TB fluid clears over 6-12 weeks; residual thickening is scar, not treatment failure.`,`An effusion **enlarging after 2 months of adherent ATT** needs re-tap, NAAT and biopsy.`,`Refer the same day for empyema, massive effusion, haemothorax or post-procedure breathlessness.`,`Refer every **unexplained exudate** after the first tap to a chest physician.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Pleural effusion drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Furosemide`,`Oral 40 mg once daily (20-80 mg); IV 40-80 mg`,`Until fluid clears`,`Hypokalaemia, AKI; check K at 1-2 weeks`],[`Spironolactone (cirrhosis)`,`Oral 100 mg with furosemide 40 mg, up to 400/160`,`Long-term`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia`],[`HRZE then HRE FDC`,`Oral, 2-6 tablets by weight band, once daily`,`2 months plus 4 months`,`Hepatitis, neuropathy, optic neuritis; pyridoxine 10 mg`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`1.2 g IV 8-hourly, then 625 mg oral TDS`,`Usually 3-6 weeks total`,`C. difficile, cholestatic hepatitis`],[`Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole`,`2 g IV daily plus 500 mg IV 8-hourly`,`Usually 3-6 weeks total`,`No alcohol with metronidazole`],[`Clindamycin (penicillin allergy)`,`600 mg IV 6-8-hourly, then 300-450 mg oral`,`Usually 3-6 weeks total`,`C. difficile colitis`],[`Alteplase plus dornase alfa`,`10 mg plus 5 mg intrapleural, twice daily`,`3 days (6 doses)`,`Pleural bleeding; both drugs together, not alone`],[`Sterile graded talc`,`4 g slurry via drain, or poudrage`,`Once`,`Pain, fever; fails if lung trapped`]]},{heading:`Light's criteria - exudate if any one`,columns:[`Test`,`Exudate if`],rows:[[`Fluid/serum protein ratio`,`Over 0.5`],[`Fluid/serum LDH ratio`,`Over 0.6`],[`Fluid LDH`,`Over two-thirds of upper normal serum LDH`],[`Correction: serum minus fluid albumin`,`Over 1.2 g/dL means transudate after diuretics`]]},{heading:`Fluid pattern by cause`,columns:[`Feature`,`Heart failure`,`Tuberculosis`,`Empyema`,`Malignancy`],rows:[[`Look`,`Clear, bilateral`,`Straw-coloured`,`Turbid or pus`,`Bloody in half`],[`Cells`,`Few mononuclear`,`Lymphocytes over 50%`,`Neutrophils`,`Lymphocytes, malignant cells`],[`Glucose and pH`,`Normal`,`Normal or mildly low`,`Glucose under 60, pH under 7.2`,`Normal or low`],[`Deciding test`,`NT-proBNP, albumin gradient`,`ADA over 40 U/L`,`pH, Gram stain, culture`,`Cytology, CT, biopsy`]]}],remember:[`Light's: protein ratio over 0.5, LDH ratio over 0.6, LDH over two-thirds ULN.`,`Diuretics fool Light's - albumin gradient over 1.2 g/dL says transudate.`,`Effusion: fremitus reduced; consolidation: fremitus increased.`,`Lymphocytic exudate with ADA over 40 in a young Indian adult is TB.`,`pH under 7.2, glucose under 60 or pus - put in a tube.`,`Never remove over 1.5 L at a sitting; never drain a hepatic hydrothorax.`,`Needle over the upper border of the rib below; drain in the safe triangle.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Pleural effusion`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Abnormal fluid in the pleural space (normal 0.1-0.2 mL/kg); **visible fluid on imaging is abnormal**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Light's criteria - exudate if any one**: fluid/serum protein over 0.5, fluid/serum LDH over 0.6, fluid LDH over 2/3 ULN serum LDH; diuretic trap - **albumin gradient over 1.2 g/dL or NT-proBNP over 1500 pg/mL = transudate**; parapneumonic - **uncomplicated / complicated (pH under 7.2, glucose under 60, LDH over 1000, Gram/culture positive, loculated) / empyema**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Transudate**: heart failure (commonest), cirrhosis (right-sided hydrothorax), nephrotic, hypoalbuminaemia. **Exudate**: in India **TB first**, parapneumonic/empyema, malignancy (lung, breast, lymphoma, mesothelioma), PE, pancreatitis, RA, SLE, drugs.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Parietal systemic capillaries filter -> lymphatic stomata drain (20x reserve) -> effusion when **hydrostatic up, oncotic down, permeability up, lymphatics blocked or transdiaphragmatic flow**; TB = **Th1 hypersensitivity -> lymphocytes, ADA**; empyema - bacterial glycolysis -> **low pH, low glucose**, fibrin septa -> peel.`],[`Key history`,`Breathlessness, pleuritic pain easing as fluid grows; **subacute fever, sweats, weight loss in a young adult (TB)**; recent pneumonia (parapneumonic); smoker over 50, haemoptysis, breast lump, asbestos (malignancy); orthopnoea, ascites, frothy urine (transudates); drugs; anticoagulants before tap.`],[`Key examination`,`**Reduced expansion, stony dullness, absent breath sounds, reduced vocal resonance**, bronchial breathing/aegophony at upper border; trachea shifted away (central or towards = collapse/fixed mediastinum); signs need **300-500 mL**; clues - clubbing, nodes, JVP, ascites.`],[`Investigations`,`**CXR** (200 mL blunts PA, 50 mL lateral); **ultrasound** (5-20 mL, septations, guides every tap); **diagnostic tap 50-60 mL**: protein, LDH with serum, glucose, **pH (heparinised syringe)**, cells, Gram/culture, cytology 25-50 mL, **ADA + Xpert Ultra** on lymphocytic exudates; **contrast CT** for unexplained exudate.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**TB: lymphocytic exudate, ADA over 40 U/L, mesothelial cells under 5%**; empyema - pH under 7.2, glucose under 60 mg/dL; RA - glucose under 30; **chylothorax TG over 110 mg/dL**; **haemothorax Hct over 50% of blood**; malignancy - cytology ~60% first sample, biopsy/thoracoscopy over 90%.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Treat cause: **furosemide 40 mg OD** for HF effusion; **spironolactone 100 + furosemide 40 mg** (up to 400/160) for hepatic hydrothorax; **TB pleurisy - 2HRZE + 4HRE by weight band, no routine steroid**; parapneumonic - antibiotics +/- drain.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Complicated parapneumonic/empyema - small-bore (12-14 F) drain + amox-clav 1.2 g IV q8h or ceftriaxone 2 g + metronidazole 500 mg q8h** (no aminoglycosides), 3-6 weeks; not resolving - **tPA 10 mg + DNase 5 mg BD x 3 days (MIST-2)**; then **VATS decortication**; malignant - **talc pleurodesis 4 g** or indwelling pleural catheter.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Ultrasound-marked site, patient leaning forward; **needle over the upper border of the lower rib**; diagnostic 50-60 mL; **therapeutic max 1-1.5 L** at a sitting, stop for cough or tightness; drain in the **safe triangle**; underwater seal below chest; **never drain a hepatic hydrothorax**.`],[`Special groups`,`Cirrhosis - repeated taps, TIPS/transplant referral, no NSAIDs; pregnancy - HRZE safe; CKD - Z and E thrice weekly; post-cardiac injury - NSAID + **colchicine 0.5 mg BD**; lupus - prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg.`],[`Complications`,`Empyema, trapped lung, residual pleural thickening (TB), **re-expansion pulmonary oedema** (over 1.5 L), pneumothorax, bleeding or organ puncture after tap, bronchopleural fistula.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`pH under 7.2/pus/loculation (drain today); massive effusion without shift (bronchoscopy); haemothorax; oesophageal rupture (pH under 7.0, high amylase); bloody effusion in smoker over 50; **effusion enlarging after 2 months of ATT**.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`TB - monthly review, **CXR at end of IP and end of treatment**, fluid clears in 6-12 weeks, residual thickening is scar; empyema - daily fever, drain output, CRP, **CT if not better at 5-7 days**; HF effusion - X-ray after 1-2 weeks diuretic.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Prevent empyema - treat pneumonia early, **pneumococcal and influenza vaccines**, dental care; TB pleurisy - **notify on Ni-kshay**, Ni-kshay Poshan Rs 1,000/month, **screen household contacts and give TPT**; asbestos control and smoking cessation; ultrasound-guided taps prevent iatrogenic pneumothorax.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**BTS 2023 pleural guideline**: **ultrasound guidance for every pleural procedure**; drain pleural infection at **pH under 7.2**; **tPA + DNase** if drainage fails; malignant effusion - **IPC or talc chosen by patient preference**; no routine pleural tumour markers.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**Any one Light criterion = exudate**; diuretics fool Light; **ADA over 40 lymphocytic exudate in a young Indian = TB**; tPA + DNase together, not alone; needle above the rib below; never more than 1.5 L; never a drain in hepatic hydrothorax.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`New pleural effusion - condensed work-up`,steps:[{label:`Effusion on CXR, confirm on ultrasound`,detail:`Bilateral with obvious heart failure - diurese first, tap only if atypical`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Ultrasound-guided diagnostic tap 50-60 mL`,detail:`Protein, LDH (with serum), glucose, pH, cells, Gram/culture, cytology, ADA, Xpert`},{label:`Apply Light's criteria`,detail:`Any one of protein over 0.5, LDH over 0.6, LDH over 2/3 ULN = exudate`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Transudate - treat HF, cirrhosis, nephrotic`,detail:`Albumin gradient over 1.2 or NT-proBNP over 1500 rescues diuretic-treated HF`},{label:`Lymphocytic exudate, ADA over 40`,detail:`TB pleurisy - 2HRZE + 4HRE, notify on Ni-kshay`,tone:`good`},{label:`pH under 7.2, glucose under 60, pus or loculated`,detail:`Chest drain + IV antibiotics; tPA + DNase if not draining`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Unexplained exudate after 2 taps`,detail:`Contrast CT then image-guided or thoracoscopic biopsy`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Parapneumonic effusion - escalation ladder`,steps:[{label:`Uncomplicated: free fluid, pH 7.2+`,detail:`Antibiotics alone (amox-clav or ceftriaxone + metronidazole)`},{label:`Complicated: pH under 7.2, glucose under 60, loculated`,detail:`Ultrasound-guided 12-14 F drain, flush 20-30 mL saline 6-8 hourly`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Empyema: frank pus`,detail:`Drain plus antibiotics for 3-6 weeks; no aminoglycosides`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Not draining at 24-72 h`,detail:`Alteplase 10 mg + dornase alfa 5 mg intrapleural BD x 3 days`},{label:`Persistent sepsis or trapped lung`,detail:`VATS decortication`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"respiratory-osa":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**OSA** is repeated collapse of the pharyngeal airway in sleep despite continued breathing effort.`,`An **apnoea** is a 90% or more fall in airflow for 10 seconds or more.`,`A **hypopnoea** is a 30% fall for 10 seconds with a 3% desaturation or arousal.`,`The **AHI** is the number of apnoeas and hypopnoeas per hour of sleep.`,`OSA means **AHI 5 or more with symptoms or comorbidity**, or 15 or more regardless.`,`About **9-13% of Indian adults** have OSA, and most are never diagnosed.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Adult severity by AHI: **mild 5-14, moderate 15-29, severe 30 or more**.`,`In children, an **obstructive AHI over 1** is already abnormal.`,`**Obesity hypoventilation syndrome**: BMI 30 or more with awake PaCO2 45 mmHg or more.`,`**Overlap syndrome** is COPD plus OSA.`,`Central apnoea has **no breathing effort**, unlike obstructive events.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Obesity**, especially a thick neck; neck size predicts OSA better than BMI.`,`**South Asians develop OSA at a lower BMI** because of a crowded craniofacial structure.`,`A small or retruded jaw, large tonsils, a large tongue and nasal obstruction.`,`Male sex and age over 50.`,`Hypothyroidism and acromegaly cause reversible OSA.`,`**Alcohol, sedatives and opioids** relax the airway and make events longer.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`The pharynx has **no bony support** and stays open only through dilator muscle tone.`,`Sleep, especially **REM sleep**, removes that tone, so the airway collapses.`,`Collapse is worst **lying supine**, as gravity pulls the tongue and palate back.`,`Effort against a closed airway creates large negative chest pressures that strain the heart.`,`Falling oxygen triggers a **sympathetic surge and brief arousal**, ending each event with a snort.`,`Repeated surges cause **resistant hypertension**, AF and insulin resistance.`,`Fragmented sleep causes **daytime sleepiness** and a two- to threefold rise in road accidents.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Interview the bed partner** about loud snoring, witnessed pauses, choking or gasping.`,`Score sleepiness with the **Epworth Sleepiness Scale**; over 10 of 24 is excessive.`,`Ask about **morning headache, nocturia**, dry mouth and unrefreshing sleep.`,`**Always ask about dozing while driving**, near-misses and commercial driving.`,`Ask about resistant hypertension, AF, diabetes, alcohol and sedatives.`,`In a child ask about snoring, mouth breathing, enuresis, hyperactivity and poor growth.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **BMI and neck circumference**; over 40 cm counts on STOP-BANG.`,`Look at the oropharynx: **Mallampati class 3-4**, large tonsils and a long uvula.`,`Check the jaw profile for **retrognathia** and the nose for septal deviation.`,`Measure BP and check the pulse for **atrial fibrillation**.`,`Raised JVP, loud P2 and oedema suggest **OHS or cor pulmonale**.`,`A normal examination **does not exclude OSA**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Screen with **STOP-BANG**: 0-2 low, 3-4 intermediate, 5-8 high risk.`,`STOP-BANG rules out; it **never makes the diagnosis**.`,`**In-lab polysomnography** is the gold standard.`,`**Home sleep testing** suits uncomplicated adults with high pre-test probability.`,`A **negative home study** in a symptomatic patient needs full polysomnography.`,`Check TSH, HbA1c and lipids; venous **bicarbonate 27 or more** needs a blood gas for OHS.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Weight loss**: a 10% loss cuts the AHI by about a quarter.`,`**CPAP is first-line** for moderate or severe OSA, and symptomatic mild OSA.`,`CPAP is a **pneumatic splint** holding the airway open; start with a nasal mask.`,`Adequate use is **4 hours or more on 70% of nights**, read from the machine download.`,`Avoid alcohol for 3-4 hours before bed, and avoid sedatives and opioids.`,`Advise **no driving while sleepy** until effectively treated.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Mandibular advancement device** for mild-moderate OSA or CPAP refusal.`,`**Positional therapy** keeps the patient on their side in supine-predominant OSA.`,`**Tirzepatide** is approved for moderate-severe OSA with obesity, up to 10-15 mg weekly.`,`Intranasal steroid treats nasal blockage and **improves CPAP tolerance**.`,`**Modafinil** only for residual sleepiness despite documented CPAP adherence.`,`**Oxygen alone is not a treatment**; it can lengthen apnoeas and raise CO2.`,`OHS needs **BiPAP** if hypoventilation predominates, plus major weight loss.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Children**: adenotonsillectomy is first-line; children are hyperactive, not sleepy.`,`**Pregnancy**: OSA raises pre-eclampsia risk, and **CPAP is safe** throughout.`,`**STOP-BANG 5 or more before surgery**: flag to the anaesthetist and minimise opioids.`,`Review new CPAP users at 1-2 weeks and 1 month, then every 6-12 months.`,`Re-study after a **weight change of 10% or more**.`,`Refer urgently for awake SpO2 under 90%, raised CO2 or dozing at the wheel.`]}],tables:[{heading:`OSA drugs at a glance`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Tirzepatide`,`SC 2.5 mg weekly, up 2.5 mg every 4 weeks to 10-15 mg`,`Long-term`,`Nausea, gallstones, pancreatitis; not with MTC or MEN2`],[`Semaglutide`,`SC 0.25 mg weekly, stepped up to 2.4 mg over 16 weeks`,`Long-term`,`Nausea, gallstones; not licensed for OSA itself`],[`Fluticasone furoate nasal`,`27.5 microgram, 2 sprays each nostril once daily`,`Continuous`,`Dryness, epistaxis`],[`Modafinil`,`Oral 100-200 mg each morning (max 400 mg)`,`While sleepy on adherent CPAP`,`Insomnia, raised BP, rare SJS; pill may fail`],[`Levothyroxine (if hypothyroid)`,`Oral 1.6 microgram/kg/day; start 25-50 in elderly`,`Long-term`,`Palpitations, angina if over-replaced`],[`Montelukast (children)`,`Oral 4 mg (2-5 y) or 5 mg (6-14 y) at bedtime`,`12-16 weeks`,`Nightmares, mood change (boxed warning)`]]},{heading:`STOP-BANG - one point each`,columns:[`Letter`,`Question`],rows:[[`S - Snoring`,`Loud snoring, heard through a closed door`],[`T - Tired`,`Often tired or sleepy in the daytime`],[`O - Observed`,`Someone saw you stop breathing or gasp`],[`P - Pressure`,`High blood pressure, treated or not`],[`B - BMI`,`Over 35 kg/m2`],[`A - Age`,`Over 50 years`],[`N - Neck`,`Over 40 cm`],[`G - Gender`,`Male`]]},{heading:`Choosing a sleep test and a treatment`,columns:[`Situation`,`Test`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Uncomplicated adult, high pre-test probability`,`Home sleep test`,`CPAP if AHI 15 or more`],[`Heart failure, COPD, opioids, stroke`,`In-lab polysomnography`,`CPAP; specialist review`],[`Obese with bicarbonate 27 or more`,`Blood gas plus polysomnography`,`BiPAP if hypoventilation`],[`Mild OSA or CPAP refusal`,`Any study confirming AHI`,`Mandibular device, positional therapy`],[`Child with snoring and symptoms`,`Paediatric polysomnography (AHI over 1)`,`Adenotonsillectomy`]]}],remember:[`STOP-BANG: Snoring, Tired, Observed, Pressure, BMI, Age, Neck, Gender.`,`STOP-BANG rules out, polysomnography rules in.`,`AHI: mild 5-14, moderate 15-29, severe 30 or more.`,`The bed partner makes the diagnosis - witnessed apnoeas, not snoring alone.`,`CPAP is a pneumatic splint, not oxygen; adequate use is 4 h on 70% of nights.`,`OSA is the commonest identifiable cause of resistant hypertension.`,`Children with OSA are hyperactive, not sleepy; AHI over 1 is abnormal.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Obstructive sleep apnoea`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Recurrent **complete (apnoea) or partial (hypopnoea) upper-airway collapse during sleep with continued effort**, ending in arousal and desaturation; **apnoea = 90%+ airflow fall for 10 s+; hypopnoea = 30%+ fall for 10 s+ with 3% desaturation or arousal**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Adult severity by AHI: mild 5-14, moderate 15-29, severe 30+/h**; children mild 1-5, moderate 5-10, severe over 10; **Epworth grades sleepiness separately (over 10 = excessive)**; OHS and overlap (COPD + OSA) are separate entities; endotypes - anatomy, muscle response, arousal threshold, loop gain.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**ICSD-3-TR: AHI (REI on home test) 5+ with symptoms (sleepiness, gasping, witnessed apnoea) or comorbidity (HT, AF, CAD, stroke, T2DM, mood disorder), or 15+ regardless**; **children: obstructive AHI over 1**; **OHS = BMI 30+ with awake PaCO2 45+ mmHg**, no other cause.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Obesity, **neck over 43 cm (men)/41 cm (women)**, male, age, retrognathia, large tonsils/tongue, nasal obstruction, alcohol and sedatives, hypothyroidism, acromegaly, PCOS; **South Asians at lower BMI**; children - adenotonsillar hypertrophy.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Unsupported pharynx + sleep loss of dilator tone (REM atonia, supine) -> **collapse above Pcrit** -> effort against closed airway (intrathoracic pressure swings) -> hypoxia/hypercapnia -> **sympathetic surge + arousal** -> hypertension, AF, insulin resistance, fragmented sleep, ANP-nocturia.`],[`Key history`,`**Bed partner: loud snoring, witnessed apnoeas, gasping**; sleepiness (**Epworth over 10**), unrefreshing sleep, morning headache, **nocturia**; **driving - dozing, near-misses, commercial driver**; resistant hypertension, AF; alcohol, sedatives; sleep duration; child - snoring, enuresis, hyperactivity.`],[`Key examination`,`BMI, waist, **neck circumference**, BP (non-dipping), irregular pulse; **Mallampati 3-4**, tonsils, uvula, retrognathia, nasal septum/turbinates; hypothyroid/acromegaly signs; **cor pulmonale or awake SpO2 under 94% = think OHS/overlap**.`],[`Investigations`,`**STOP-BANG** (0-2 low, 3-4 intermediate, **5-8 high**) screens only; **level 1 polysomnography** is gold standard; **home sleep apnoea test** for uncomplicated high-probability adults (negative -> PSG); TSH, HbA1c, lipids, Hb, ECG; **venous HCO3 27+ -> ABG** for OHS.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**CPAP/APAP (4-20 cmH2O)** for **AHI 15+** or symptomatic mild OSA; nasal mask or pillows first, heated humidifier, ramp; **adherence 4 h+/night on 70% of nights** from download; **weight loss 5-10%+** (10% loss = ~25% AHI fall), alcohol and sedative avoidance.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Mandibular advancement device** (mild-moderate, CPAP refusal); **positional therapy** for supine OSA; **tirzepatide 2.5 mg SC weekly, +2.5 mg every 4 weeks to 10-15 mg** for moderate-severe OSA with obesity (FDA 2024); bariatric surgery BMI 35+ (32.5+ Asians); **BiPAP for OHS/overlap**; modafinil 100-200 mg for residual sleepiness on adherent CPAP.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Machine below head level, **distilled/boiled water humidifier**, fit mask sitting up, straps just tight enough, ramp, **use all night every night**; wash cushion daily, tubing weekly, replace cushion 3-6 months; fixes - humidifier, nasal steroid, chin strap, nasal pillows.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy: CPAP safe**, stop tirzepatide/semaglutide, avoid modafinil; **children - adenotonsillectomy first-line**, montelukast/nasal steroid for mild or residual; **perioperative - STOP-BANG 5+ flag**, minimise opioids, own CPAP; HFrEF with central apnoea - **no ASV**.`],[`Complications`,`**Resistant hypertension**, AF, stroke, coronary disease, heart failure, type 2 diabetes, **road accidents (2-3x)**, depression, pulmonary hypertension (with OHS/COPD), perioperative respiratory events.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Dozing at the wheel**, awake SpO2 under 90-94%, HCO3 27+ or PaCO2 45+, cor pulmonale, worsening drowsiness on oxygen/sedatives; refer for PSG, OHS, central apnoea, narcolepsy, CPAP failure; ENT for nasal obstruction and every symptomatic snoring child.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`CPAP review **1-2 weeks, 1 month, 3 months, then 6-12 monthly** - download (hours, residual AHI under 5-10, leak), Epworth, weight, BP; **re-study after 10%+ weight change**, surgery or MAD titration.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Weight control from youth (South Asians get OSA at lower BMI); avoid evening alcohol and sedatives; **screen high-risk groups** - resistant hypertension, AF, T2DM, obesity, pregnancy with hypertension, **commercial drivers**, pre-operative (STOP-BANG); treat rhinitis; ENT review for every snoring child.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**FDA December 2024: tirzepatide** - first drug approved for **moderate-severe OSA with obesity** (SURMOUNT-OSA, AHI fall ~25-30/h); **ICSD-3-TR (2023)** diagnostic criteria; AASM scoring v3 keeps hypopnoea = 30% fall + 3% desaturation or arousal.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**STOP-BANG rules out, PSG rules in**; **oxygen alone is not OSA treatment**; CPAP is a pneumatic splint; OSA = commonest identifiable cause of resistant hypertension; **children are hyperactive, not sleepy**; AHI over 1 abnormal in children.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Snorer to treatment - OSA pathway`,steps:[{label:`Snoring, witnessed apnoeas, sleepiness`,detail:`Epworth over 10; ask about driving; resistant HT or AF`},{label:`STOP-BANG`,detail:`0-2 low, 3-4 intermediate, 5-8 high risk of moderate-severe OSA`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Sleepy driver?`,detail:`Advise no driving now, fast-track study`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Home study if uncomplicated, else PSG`,detail:`Negative home study with symptoms - full polysomnography`},{label:`Grade by AHI`,detail:`Mild 5-14, moderate 15-29, severe 30+; HCO3 27+ -> ABG for OHS`,tone:`decision`},{label:`CPAP for AHI 15+ or symptomatic mild`,detail:`Plus weight loss; MAD or positional therapy as alternatives; BiPAP for OHS`},{label:`Review download: 4 h+ on 70% of nights`,detail:`Residual AHI under 5-10, Epworth normal`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`OSA treatment ladder`,steps:[{label:`Lifestyle for all`,detail:`Weight loss 5-10%+, no evening alcohol or sedatives, sleep hygiene, treat rhinitis`},{label:`Positional therapy or MAD`,detail:`Supine-predominant or mild-moderate OSA, CPAP refusal`},{label:`CPAP/APAP`,detail:`First line for AHI 15+ or symptomatic mild OSA; nasal interface first`},{label:`Tirzepatide for moderate-severe OSA with obesity`,detail:`2.5 mg SC weekly, +2.5 mg every 4 weeks to 10-15 mg; not in pregnancy, MTC or MEN2`},{label:`BiPAP/NIV`,detail:`OHS, overlap syndrome, CPAP intolerance at high pressure`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Surgery`,detail:`Adenotonsillectomy (children first line); bariatric surgery BMI 35+ (32.5+ Asians)`,tone:`decision`}]}]}};export{e as default};